      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>ONCE 2 MC f )01-TGAL LS pLITEN T SELF-RAISING FLOUR MP USED. LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>ONCE 2 MC f )01-TGAL LS pLITEN T SELF-RAISING FLOUR MP USED. LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. IT takes a tough sail to stand the wind of pros- perity. On of the striking characteristics of the new woman is her aversion to old clothes,</p>
        <p>" POVERTY is no crime," for sometimes it doesn't even furnish one with prison fare. THE woman who thinks before she speaks has to be a lightning thinker. Jr is easier to forgive success in any one else than a relative. _ _ _</p>
        <p>" Ws&amp; steals my purse. steals trash ; " but the ,cry of "Stop thief !"is raised jest the same. TOMMY: "Paw, what is an empty nothing ? " .Mr. Figg : "I don't know, unless it is a dude before breakfast." TOO DENSE FOR Hl7,l.—Stage Villain: " Aha ! The plot thickens." Disgusted Auditor: "Then rin off! Can't make head or tail of it now, and I'll never see. through it if it gets any thicker." ORGANS ALL RIGHT.—Young Wife: "Don't you think my husband's ill-health is due to weak diges- tive organs ?"' Doctor: "No—oh, no; nothing but bad cooking." A DAILY ASSISTANT.—Friend: "Does your wife wife assist you any in your work? I often see her at your desk." humorist: " Y-e-s, she goes over all I write, and burns all my jokes about wives." HE : "Wasn't Brown's wife named Stone before she was married?" She: " Yes ; and it was a very suitable name." He: "What do you mean ?" She: "Oh, nothing; only 'she threw herself at his head." MRS. HIGGINS (recounting a row with Mrs. 'Awkins) : "'She says to me, she says, You're no lady.' Say that agin,' I says, say I ain't no lady, and I'll pull all the 'air off yer " " BRIDGET, have you cracked those nuts for the dessert I want to make ?" " Yis, ma'am; all but thin big walnuts, an' it'll take stronger jaws than mine to manage them; but I got trOo wid do others all.roight, ma'am." 0 CHANCE Fpit : "I wonder why li,ihtning never strikes twice in the same place." I.‘" agleigh : "Well, you can't generally find the place."</p>
        <p>- HOLINESS OF NEWNESS.—LittIe Nell: " 0-o-o I'm going to tell mamma !" -Little Ned: " Woty's I (loin' now ? 7 Little Nell: "You're wipin' your feet on zee new door-mat 1"</p>
        <p>A RARE CHANCE. —Jack Borrowit "I awoke last nigh, and found a beggar in my room." George Genrus "Well, well! Did you succeed in borrowing anything from him." HE QUALIFIED HIS PRAISE.—MTS. Twicken- ham: "Your daughters, colonel, are remarkably fresh-looking girls." Colonel Gunner: "Yes, they are. You wouldn't believe, madam, that the eldest of those girls is"—(nudged by his wife)— "growing younger every year.' "M&amp;, I want a pony. Can't I have a pony, Ma?" "Certainly, my son, I suppose so. Ask your father." "I don't like to ask him, ma." "Why, what nonsense! Ask him." "No, ma; you ask him; you've known him the longest ! " THE EYES HAVE Xl'.—" The eye is a sure indicator of character," said Dawson. "The man with a stony eye is a hard, stern individual; the avid eye .shows an avid nature, and so on." "Nonsense !" retorted Jarley. "One of the merriest girls I ever knew was blue-eyed, and the most amiable man I ever war cross-eyed." _ _</p>
        <p>THE VOICE OF TRIIIIIIII.—Mrs. Kidby : "Oh, John dear, don't you hear? Haw delightfully the baby crows !" Kidby : " Crows ?" Humph! I'd crow mykelf if I were boss of the house,"</p>
        <p>RECOGN-ISED HIS HANDIWORK.-Mr.. Gusher (a would-be suitor): "What lovely teeth Miss Smiler has—eh, old man?" Mr. Forceps: " H'm, er— really—modesty forbids my expressing an opinion, you know."</p>
