        <p>PHRENOLOGY SCORES AGAIN.—" This bump," said the phrenologist, feeling Cadley's hcad, " shows that you are fond of outdoor life." "You bet it does," replied Cadley. "I got that bump from falling off a bicycle while indulging in my love for outdoor life." JONES-BROWN "Why do you go into society ? " Brown-Jones: "To find a wife. And you ?" Jones-Brown: "To get away from one." "THEE ARE TRlCKS."—Visitor : "Are all these ladies waiting for change.?" Merchant Prince: " Oh, dear, no! They are connected with the holm. They stimulate trade by struggling with customers who try to approach the bargain .counter." HE RELENTED.—" Draw!" cried the knight, .advaneing' with hand on sword. "Draw, in the name of chivalry !" He paused. "Craven !" he sneered. His face softened. "Perhaps he can't," he muttered. "Perhaps he's an impressionist."</p>
        <p>A HORSE-DEALER, in showing off a spirited nag to a customer, received a kick in the side, awl, although smarting under the pain, made up the best face he could, and exclaimed. "Playful crea- ture, isn't he ? "</p>
        <p>HE was asking the old man for his daughter in marriage. Ile was talking tremblingly, hesita- tingly, as you read of in story books. It came the old man's turn to speak, and as he began his face was white with passion, and his voice shook with excitement. "You want to marry my daughter ? " he said. "Ah ! Twenty years ago your father crippled me in a stock deal, and I swore to be revenged. And now my time has come." He paused for breath, and the aspirant for the maiden's hand was about to beat a hasty retreat in the face of supposed defeat when the father broke forth again : "Yes, sir, I swore to be revenged, and I'll now strike the 7ather through the son. Want my daughter—eh? Well, take her, and may she prove as expensive to you as she has to me." The old man dropped into his chair, worn out with the excitement of his plot, and the young man fainted. AT LAST. —Telegraph Editor: "By Jove, here's an account of the sea-serpent being seen off the Florida coast, and I believe it's true this time." City Editor: "Why more than before ? " Tele- graph Editor: "Great Scott, man, don't you notice that in no place does it say that it had a head like a barrel, or that it raised itself out of the water and hissed horribly when shot at ? "</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I have seen some pretty girls wearing some pretty and youthful hats of dead white coarse straw, lined with the palest pink crepe-de-chine. Round the top of the big high crown, and round the base were two wreaths of pale pink rose blossoms, a plume of white feathers stood up at one side, and a soft puffing of white chiffon clouded the brim. I have also seen another rather fascinating fashion, but one which only a few particularly pretty or particularly courageous people have yet taken to, is to wear a huge high-crowned rustic straw hat wreathed with roses and with wide strings of pale pinli, blue or white tulle tied under the chin or crossirg under the hair at the back, and then brought round under the chin.</p>
        <p>A pretty paletot for a little girl is in sage- green Pashmin cloth; short waist, double-breasted. Pointed collar and cuffs, embroidered in silk. Girdle of silk cord. The paletot is lined with brocade. The hat is in tintoretto silk to match, with full bind; a Tudor crown and ostrich plumes. A becomimg blouse is made of grass lawn, with pale blue and yellow stripes in the material. The vest, waistband, and collar are of blue satin ribbon, and the full front is draped over with double revers of lawn, which are edged with lace. This charm- ing bodice can be worn with any coloured stripes and trimmings to match.</p>
        <p>For blouses, the most popular material is grass. lawn, both plain and elaborate. For dressy wear a pretty blouse has simulated braces formed of three rows of pink baby ribbon darned through the coarsely-woven material, the sleeves being striped down the outer side with two seta of ribbons— three rows in each set. Another way of arranging the ribbon is as a yoke inste7d of braces. Very smart, too, is a blouse of 3 most transparent kind of grass-lawn lined with pale rose-pink, with sleeves ending mid-way between elbow and wrists in frills of dcru chiffon, and a vandyked grass-lawn collar lined with rose and trimmed with chiffon to match the wrist ruffles.</p>
        <p>A most becoming costume is of blue piquoi, with small white spots on it, or it might, be made in any other coloured material with Nt hite spots, and in white and pale shades with darker spots, as well as in every plain-coloured pique. 'The coat is well cut and the material is of the best quality. The excessive heat has naturally made us long for cool, unlined blouses, such as t.re worn in the morning at home or in the country, but are scarcely dressy enough for afternoon wear. Nowadays the blouse is tightly lined and boned, and is very little cooler than an ordinary bodice of thin material. Plain or spotted grass lawn, or lawn with tiny coloured silk stripes, or any of the more sub- stancial of the silk zephyrs, made of delightfully using piece guipure for the yoke and cuffs, and lace in in two widths for the other trimming. The heavier and less expensive grass L%IL llaUSt not be used, as the transparent silky make is too thin to be made without lining. The blouse is cut quite plain and high, but there is very little ful- ness at the neck, and the piece guipure is laid over the lawn to simulate a yoke, and the edge stitched down to the lawn. Lace, five- or six inches wide, is used for the frill, and two and a- half yards will be sufficient for an ordinary figure. This should be divided in half, but not cut, and again into quarters, leaving the latter to go over the sleeves to its full width, but gradually sloping thb lace narrower towards the back and front. The blouse should be cut of the right length to reach the waist without straining, and yet without any bagginess, and the edge is set into a folded band of lawn of the right size to fit the waist. Your loving</p>
        <p>where they will remain for about a fortnight; the Prin- cess will then go to Copenhagen with Prince'and Prin- cess Charles of Denmark, and Princess Victoria of Wales. It seems probable that the Princess of Wales will remain in Denmark until the end of October, instead of going to Scotland.</p>
        <p>Monday last, was the anniversary of the birthday of Her Royal Highness Princess Victoria of Wales. The Princess who is the second daughter of the Prince and Princess of Wales, will be the only member left at home when Princess Maud's marriage is an accomplished fact. Princess Victcria, like her elder 'sister, the Duchess of Fife, has a great affection for the natural beauty and simplicity of rural life. She is exceedingly fond of animals, is an expert horsewoman, and her graceful figure may frequently be seen following the hounds. Many people do not seem to be aware of the fact that the heir aprarent to the British throne has not been Prince of Wales all his life. The eldest son ef the reign- ing sovereign is not born Prince of Wales, that title be- ing the subject on each occasion of a fresh patent. George IV. was created Prince of Wales five days after his birth,but the present Prince was three weeks old be- fore the creation took p!ace. During those three weeks he bore the only title to which the eldest son of the reigning sovereign is alone entitled by birth, viz. Duke of Cornwall.</p>
        <p>e... The appointmenrt of Professor Bridge as successor to Sir Joseph Barnby at the Albert Hall, London, is a judi- cious one,and has been received with general satisfac- tion. Dr. Bridge is one of the leading figuree in the musical world of London—but his work extends far be- yond it. In addition to his functions at Westminster Abbey he is a Professor at the Royal Academy of Music, and Examiner in no less than three Universities. He throws his whole soul into everything he does and his meed of work is no light one. The " Sign of the Croes " still draws large crowds to the Lyric Theatre night by night, and the union of Church and Stage for which the Rev. Stewart Headlam so earnestly contended,seems to be an accomplished fact if one can judge by the number of clerical gentlemen who nightly form a large and appreciative porticn of the audience. It appears Mr. Wilson Barrett Will shortly retire from the "Sign of the Cross," for a. while to com- plete his new play "The Daughters of Babylon." Mr. Cooper Cliffs will replace the Liric manager as Marcus Superbus. iMiss Annie B. Swan, the well-known Scotch novelist, sell') has written in all some 30 novels among which are " The Gates of Eden,"" Sheila," " A Bitter Date," was born at Leith, the Furth of seven children. Her father was a great agriculturist, and her mother a woman of practical common sense, who believed in all her daughters being well trained in domestic duties. Miss Swan indeed got no hereditary tendency to literature from either side. At fifteen she commenced to write short stories, the novelist's own temperament lea6ing her in general to a happy ending. Mr. Glad•tone is a particular admirer of her latest story " A Victory Won," and the ex-Premier in an autograph letter to the author said her book was" beautiful as a work of art." 'rho question of oar sex overcrowding the Terrace of the Houses of Parliament for afternoon tea, which has for months past agitated the minds of several distin- guished members, is to be left over till next year when something is to be done. It always reminds me, when anything is te be " left over " in this uncertain condi- tion, of the people who are always taking about moving into a new house. This is usually going to happen next year% or sooner, but never later, and, as matter af fact it is just this class of talkative people who go on year after year in their old quarters. That official of the House of Commons, the gallant Sergeant at Arms, evidently sympathises with those members of Parliament who regard the Terrace parties as the most pleasant part of their duties, and he declines all responsibility in the matter. The other day Sir W. Lawson wanted to know whom he could prosecute for the supposed illegal sale of refreshment in the unlicensed Parliamentary premises, but nobody could tell him. Ap- parently such matters are insuperable difficulties to Parliament, but if it is really desired to exclude us freer the Terrace, if the Gevernment or others will find the responsible official, any one of the women visitors could tell him that the simplest way would be to throw it open for a time to the unfashionable multitude. The trouble would then disappear. The fashi ,n of fichus has developed to such an extent that every secend or third woman one meets just now, is wearing one. There is no doubt that they are a charm- ing finish to pretty gowns, worn by slight and graceful women, bet there is also no possible doubt that they are very much the teverse when donned by women who lack both grace and height. Dainty evening blouses for wearing at table d'hote, and when at the seaside are occupying the thoughts of those cf us who are about to take our holidays. A good plan is to provide ourselves with a plain slip bodice with sleeves, of some pretty bright coloured silk, and then run up a few blouses of spotted muslin, greradinei flowered, or striped grass lawn, which with a fichu of the same should be quite separate from the silk foundation. The sash worn with each of theFe blouses should if possible'repeat the colour of the muslin and that of the skirt. A black silk and satin skirt is the most useful for evening wear when economy and blewies are the order of the day. The fourth of July as we all well know, is Independ- ence Day, but however independent American citizens may be, there is no reason why they should disregard the tradition which makes the Royal Standard the pecu- liar badge of Royalty. One American woman, resident in London, who gave a very magnificent reception on this particrCar day, issued invitation cards on which the , Stars and Stripes were entwined, not with the Union Jack, but win) the Hoye) Standard. This lady although efie halm from A.uneriera e.as yet apparentl, something to learn. Madame Melba'e passien for old furniture, Lord Walsingbam's for moths and butterflies and the late Lord Lilforcl's for birds, sink into significance before the callossal hobby of a leader of New York society. This energetic woman's one idea seems to be—teapots—and already she has amassed a collection of over a thousand examples, varying in capacity from three gallons to about a thimbleful. A eads, figures, birds, animals, fi hoe, beetles, reptiles, in copper, silver, and in every kind of pottery, and in every. colour, turn out to be receptacles for the fragrant beverage, and the fair enthusiast who has spent some years in Japan in pursu- ance of her taste,' has reason to be proud of her treasures. _ .</p>
        <p>. . The" New Woman "is certainly making rapid strides</p>
        <p>_ . in Australia! At a Woman's Suffrage meeting in Mel- bourne some little time back one advanced individual</p>
        <p>observed in the course of a very spirited speech, that "they had already got doctors in petticoats for which they ought to be thankful, but it yet remained for them to get magistrates and judges in petticoats." After care• Fully se udying the lady's speech which by the way is certainly not lacking in length, I am however in a state of uncertainty as to whether it is the pe'ticoats for which we should he thankful, or the lady doctors. Here is an exceront way in which to use up cold bniled rice. Take oie dish and spread at the bottom a layer of any kind of jam. Take the cold rice and beat up well with a fork. Add slowly, some cold milk the yolk of an egg whhh has been well beaten, a little sugar and a few drop. of I.mon or vanilla essence. Pour the mixture, which nvlst not be too thick, into the pie dish and bake in a cptiok oven for about fifteen minutes. When done, well whl,k the white of the egg,mix with it a tablespoonful of castor sugar, and heap roughly on the top. Return it to the oven for about ten minutes, and serve at once. " MADGE."</p>
