        <p>Her Majesty will, according to present arrangements only spend two days in London, for on the day follow- ing Princess Maud's wedding the Queen goes direct to Osborne, where elaborate re-decoration has been carried out. A greater part of the house has, it appears, never been touched since it was first decorated. It is well knovir.i that Her Majesty has a great dislike to seeing anything new about her, and as Osborne is her own private property she has only to consider her own tastes in the matter.</p>
        <p>At the end of the month the Prince and Princess of Wales with Princess Victoria will proceed to Cowes</p>
