      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>'VIMASCE.</head>
        <p>'VIMASCE.</p>
        <p>• COMIC:IIOrIte,* GCEEN in moving the adoptien of the minutes Of the Finance and Estates Committee, referred with srtisfaction to the report of the auditors of the borough accounts. Et must, he said, be satisfactory to' everyone to ',know that even a prnfessional auditor like Councillor Bennett sea.s-satiefied with the accounts. The Meroet seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor Losiorom said an interesting report hal been read by the Chairman at 4a, arnmittee meeting, acd he suggested that they might have the report read to the Council. There was considerable feeling with regardto deputations bothin the Connell and out, and the report would show that deputations were of some value. The MAYOL suggested that the report might be herded to the press so Witt the couricillore and others meght read it at their ewn firesides, and this suggestien was agreed to. The ince is as follows ee24l'n accordance with' your instructions. the Mayor, the BeroughTreasurer,ate. my- self (Couneilloreehaw Green) attended the annual meet- ing of the Borough Treasurers"' Association at Brighton, on Thursday and 'Friday, theriBth and /9th June. At 'ten o'clock sharp on Thursday morning, a largeattend- ance of the menhers and of the Mayors and Chairman of Finance Committees answered the call of the 'President. The assemblrevas welcomed to Brighton by the Mayor. who placed at the disposal of essoziation the beauti- ful suite-of rooms known as the Pavillon. In a spirited address the 'President reminded the members that though -in their individnel capacity they might be considered parochial, yet collectively and in the magni- tude of their operations they bece:ne national. By such gathering e they were enabled to test the value of the various modes of ,financing, and each wohld get the benefit of the experience of other Corporations, and thereby they would 'fit thensselves for the `totter dis- charge of the:r duties and or the increasing responsibili- ties and burdens that were pressing eupon the finances 'ef Corporations. Special reference was made to -the inability ye unwillingness of the Local Government !Board to grasp 'and deal firmly with. the overseership equestions raised by the Bill of ' 18S4. Important statements were made with reference to an extension of the Trustees' ,Act 1893., and the conference gave considerable time to a con- sideration of its effect upon small Corporations, and the meeting was of opinion there was no reason why the, Act should not be extended to the smaller boroughs. We emphasieedthe advisability of keeping ,nut•of this pos- sible alteration the securities known as annuities. Were these included, as our ow-sr-treasurer pleated out, it would result in a very serious increase in the value of our gas annuities. Matters incidentally referred te were the rating of the public parks, the unfair operation of the income tax and the tiistribution 'of the beer money by the county authorities, many 'of the non- county boroughs, like ourselwee, smarting under deo action of the County Councils. Thefinanoial clauses of the new Education Bill, under which, icl-passed, the' whole of tieisauoney must beepent, werebroughtbefore our notice. these powers are conferred there will bee a most importE.nt increase in the duties of 'Corporations; and though the-Bill is now withdrawn itmeas well our attention was called to it. .1 very full and important-, debate toekplace upon the question of the conversion of irredeemable securities. This was one of the burning questions, andtie, was soon apparent that a good many, Corporationfebad, like ourselves, a millstone round their necks. It will be seen from the•follewine that a solu- Ition easy and 4ree from injury to the holders of such is I possible, and it ',would be a wise thing if eha Associa- tion of Municipal Corporationa,,erould briog it under the notice of their representatives. The alterna- tire .propositions with respect to theee, were as follows e-ea. tenet a term of eay•so years -be granted , to annuitants and irredeemable 'stock holder*" in which to redeem so that' the market pica of their securities would not be effected; 'That the Local Govern- meat 'Board should fix a price at which the securities should be.redeemed I or; 3, That4the Corporatien should I be given power to compulsorily ,purchase each year at a fixed priceloasellon an averageeief the threeprevieus years' prices so much stock and annuities as will meet the requirements of their sinking funds. &amp; very lengthy diseustion eneuedupon a paper by Mr:Swainson, of Bolton.upen thelquestion of short terms v. stock. 'lt was an able debate, in which all sorts of theories were raised, but no conclusion satisfactory to Corpwations generally was reacheci,;(or the want Of that prophet who cereld say what was to be the state:cf the money u.arket twenty-five yeses hence. Other matters before the con- ference were the consolidation of -rate collectiote and upon this a propositionkteas carried that the executive take all necessary steps to get the lull powers of- the Act of 1894, se as to give municipalities the powers in- tented by that Act. Nome tax assessments and other kin Ced subjecte,of.an educational character mere de- bated, and it was interesting to notice how ready the older hands were to giveeinformation to -the younger. The difficulties that some Corporations had got into were explained, so that they might beemoided: ; and the conclusion arrived at by the deputation was that by this gatheiCing those responsible for tho,ntonagement of the finances of Corporations were all strengthened; helped, and better fitted for fulfilling the increallipgly oneresa duties imposed upois them. The minutes were then adapted. THE fir.ll)l/H, 4E' aTaEBTiI. Alderman SUTTON moved the confirmation of minutet. of the Street Improvement Cozamittee, sase of the ree commendations of the Committee being that the sum of £lOO be paid to the Wiarringtan Equitable aneustrial Co-operative Society, LAUeitede for the purchase of 'land 4for street improvements at the corner of 'Tonna-at. and Church-st. Aldermaa BLecirsuemer seconded. Councillor 131INNETT said he thought it only right to print out that if the plan of the (Co-operative Society's blending in Church-Bt. be -carried out the eCorporation would be permanently preventing itself from carrying out improvements for the "Adenine of the street. Alderman eceremg said they bad arranged for the street to be:Boft. wide. •Aldezman BIACKHIrRST also said they bad secured o line which would straighten the street. Councillor Beunewier said the street would not be straightened as it would be at an angle. He proposed, as an amendment that this part of the minutes be not accepted. The aenenclment was, however, not seconded. Alderman BOLTOV defended the action-of the Committ- ee and aaid that to make any other arrangement 'would came a great deal of expense. Alderman Serrox,,in reply, sa;cl the Gemmittee had made the best arrangements they could. WAVING LIU SEWERAGE COMMITML—THIC 'WORK OF IME</p>
        <p>,COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT asked the chairman,of this Com- mittee the fogowing cinestion of which he had given notice :—" When the levelling and paving of Gi.bson- stre, New-street, and °Ober streets and passages in the vicinity are likeig to be completed ; whether the atten- tion of the committee hastbeen called to the unsatisfac- tory condition ck the adjacent brook.; and whethcr, having segard to tke offensive smells which continue to proceed fzom some of our local sewers, the corsznittea avematle any file:Ler enquiries as to tfne feasibility of ,ventilating them by crnacionta shafts or chimneys, or of otherwise obviating the nuiss,nce." .Alderman WRIGHT, in replying, said notice had been given for the &amp;evening and paving of Gibson-street. tie was not aware of any nuisance from the brook, hut 44f !there was Councillor Bennett shotrid apply to .4he Health Committee. As te the ,question of ventilating the sewers thes., had talked out the matter a ereat deal bat their engineer did not np to the present aeivise them to adopt the shafts. Re then moved the adoption of the Commit- tees minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CARD</head>
        <p>A CARD</p>
        <p>NOWN as Rev. Joseph Holmes' Mexican Prescription I-1- is the only original guaranteed Remedy for all those who suffer from nervous weakness, exhausted vitality. kidney or bladder diseases, gravel, prostate, spinal weak- ness, brain fag and kindred complaints. It has stood the test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed stamped nvelope to Jog. HoLstza' Remedy Co. 13loomsbury Man eions, Bloomsbury square, London, and get f ull particulars of this great specific free of charge. Mention whether for male or female. Beware of fraudglent imitators.</p>
        <p>Councillor BEATON seconded. Councillor HEFRERD asked for some explanation of the proposed paving of Wilderspool Causeway and Latchford-read, with regard to the new Arpley-road he thought the committee had made a good jab' of it. Councillor LONOYON referred to accounts for £1,853 and was informed that the bulk of this moneF had been spent on street making aud would be refunded by pro. perty owners. Alderman FRANCOMR was glad to see that they were about to proceed with the new sewer at Stockton Heath. It was, he said almost intolerable living up there. He was also pleased to see that the committee were spend- ing so much money on the paving of back passages. Alderman WRIOEIT said he was gratified to hear the approval of the work of his committee. Ho also men- tioned that the committee were determined to go on making permanent roads and do away with macadam as soon as possible. He then 'read the follewing report of the Borough Surveyor on the werks of tke committee: " Hexing been called hurriedly te London re Ship Canal Billj wish to report that the work of ytrur department is vrogres,ing with all speed,and since yeur last meeting Tanner's-lane has been 'finishea and tbrown open for traffic. lit'ilderspool Causeway has been open for traffic over a week,and we are now engaged on the short por- tion between Old-road and 'Railway...crossings, which 'I expect to have finished and thrown onen for traffic on Thursday next. Latchford-road': Nearly the whole 'length to Clifton-terrace on one half the road will be finished and thrown opsn'for traffaz on Thursday next. Padgatelanee Tiling has been oomthenced from fad- gate Bridge ; both footpaths near the bridge are'finished and the tiling half way to the King and'Queen'lnn corn. Pleted on the cyst Aide. Winwith-road : The new Ene of curb from Jockey-street is in progress and laid a con- siderable length towards Longford Bridge. The tiling vett be 'Commenced as soon as curb is complete and 'road made good. Bowes-street, Jones=s-buildings, and seven adjoining passages,: 'These works are well in hand, and will be compieted in about 'ten daysj tourteen passages round and about Sankey-st,, and /Arpley-st:: The contractors are commencing Meese works after Walking Day. Stevenson-Bt.': This street coin- meneed to-day, and will ba pushedon with all speed. 'New road across Arptey': We are dill workin; on tli.s road, but expect to have it complete on Thersday next. think ale cost wlll be slightly under the amount 're- receivedlrom the Ship Canal Company when compliete. Wharf': This is progressing satisfactorily., but rather slowly but the contractor has got over his principal difficulties, and will now be abflo to go on with nore speed. Sankey-st., and Palmyra-square; I proptae to comtncnce paving next liondky, as I did not consider it 'wise to upset the-streets !Defer° Walking Day. C. have sent &amp;Wiz plans, estimates, 'and provisional apportion- taenti for nine passages between Lyttigees-lane, 'hardy- at., and Chorley-st., which kindly pass." The MAYOR said he thought they would all be obliged ' to 'Alderman Wright for having taken the trouble to give them that information. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>twe sztv cLio.</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of the minutes of the School Attendance Committee. CoEncillor Saw iGlarrN said the committee intended to visit the ship " Clio " as an earnest of their responsibility they felt for the boys sent , to the ship. He was sorry to say there were a good many , 'candidates for the • " Clio.'' At the last meeting of the: cmumittee they had no fewer than five boyebeforethent leklose parents said they'were beyond theirfcontrol, and two of these boys wererunder 10 years. They should want two " Clios ' if thic thing went on, sod they were grateful they had such an institution to which boys scald be sent at so small a cost. (Hear' bear.) Councillor SHILLCOCK t3conded, and the' minutes were -confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1896.</p>
        <p>class of people who don't read the local papers. There are some qualities of bricks that have to be bought from particular firms, because none other will do. Councillor ROBERTS : That i$ nonsense. Alderman BOLTON : That is what the Gas Committee are told by their engineer, and in a question of that kind, I would take his opinion before yours. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS lam not speaking for myself in this matter, but on behalf of the general body of ratepayers. Alderman BOLTON And lam representing a body of ratepayers. You should move that the Standing Orders be altered. To backbite the engineer, and to try to foist the blame upon him for what the committee has done is out of the question altogether. We are responsible, and not Mr. Haddock ; and it is hardly fair that the Gas Engineer, who has no right to speak here,should be thus charged. Ceuncillor ROBERTS The Gas Engineer has no right to 'come here. &amp;Weimar:). BovroN : You have no right to make charges against a man who cannot answer them. I con- tend that the committee has done perfectly right in faccepting the tender. It was the best they had. Councillor Roman : Bet you don't give the local 'tradesmen a chance of tendering. Alderman BOLTON : I shall support the comemittee,and I hope that the Council will do so. He hoped the resoka- tion trespecting tenders would be altered shortly, and that all local papers and no others shoed be adver- tised in.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR:: I. think the discuesion has gone quite far enough. Councillor Stew GREEN asked whether any reason cenld be given why the tender was enty advertised in the paper mentioned. Councillor 'Roberts had raised the question in a proper spirit and in a good temper. The question was, was the oentract ilet in accordance 'with the spirit of the resolution? Alderman BOLTON:: It has been. Councillor HEPHERD in reply, said accordieg to the reading of the resolution of 11887, the committee were quite within their province in advertising in whatever paper they liked. The local newspapers vrere not defined in the resolution Counctalor Roncater: Not specifically. That is quite true. Councillor HEPIEERD said that if the extemittee had acted vrongly the local papers should be specified in the resonation. The MAYOR: .s7.ou would be quite willing for the tenders to be inserted in the local papers:? Csuncillor &amp;MEETS : If the Chairman will give that assurance I will move a resolution at tire next meeting. Alderman Some: Mr. Eepherd cannot pledge the committee- the MAYOR.: Well, it is for you tevote upon it. Councillor BEMIRED continuing, said he was sorry Councillor Rcezerts had taken this matter up because of his connection with the trade inquestion. Many tenders tad been brought before the committee by Mr. Haddock, and they had advertised over and over again in the local •newspapers and had never had a sine reply. Theuom- ,enittee advertised in theiocal papers for the tender that , 'Messrs. Pearson and Knewles had get, and they aisoTut in an applitation for a tot of timber. With the decep- tion of Meters. Pearson and Knowles there was no other tender. e did not thiak that the particular tender of Morton's was brought before the committee, but lir. Haddock had often referred to it, and he was sorry Councillor Roberts had introduced the name of the en- gineer into the matter, because he (Councilor Repiterd) and hie commiteee took up )n themselves all the respon- sibility of the tenders. Mr. Ilsiedock had often-asked "Shall E put this in the Warrington papers ? " and the reply heti been "-No:" Therevetre only two Warrington firms tha could have sent a tender. COUHLMOT FIJEWEE3 : Four. Councillor HEMMED Well,lour. One would think that it would be to their advantage to take .papers in which Urge tenders are advertised in. Mr. Haddock was nct to blame in this matter. Councillor Rompers said he did not wish to Tress the Council to a division, but at the next meeting he would bring forward a motion to *amend the reselation to whictehe had referred, to give each committee an op- portunity of advertising in the local papers and any other paper they wished. He would withdraw his amendment. Tiec minutes were then agreed to. WLRRIN OTON AND TELE -SHIP CANAL BILL.</p>
        <p>THE • ACTION OF THE TIMMS' COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Carmelllor ROEERTS moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Rivers' Comenittee,and said in the face of the very important decision of the Lords' 'Committee given the other day, the Council would no doubt ex- pect him to say a word or two in regard to it. In ac- cordance with the second resolution on page 12 of the Confacil Summons—the Mayor, the Town Clerk. the Borengh-Surveyor and himself attended on the sLorehe Committee last week, and they were profoundly dis- appointed with the decision which the Lords' Commit- tee had given. Before referring to that decision, how- ever, he wouldlike to referto an article Which had ap- peared in the"-Manchester Guardian " of Wonday last from a correspondent. , It-was headed" The Lords and the-Chip Canal:2 and it put the position of Warrington., to his mind, its a very inaccurate and unfair manner. 'There were one or two sentences he wouldoquoteoramely: —"The Bill enabling the. Ship Canal Company to close The CAM openings,as awaited by the Lords' amendments affords practically everything required for °fthip Canal purposes, but takes away -advantages which the Billaa it left the Lower Elsallse would have conferred upon War- rington, Runcorn. Widnes, and'Ellesmere /Fort. It was a shett-sighted policy on the part of these districts, and in the case of Wldnes andel7arrington to serve their in- terests by improving the navigation of the upper estuary, in which they are both in. terested." As .Chairman of the Rivers' 'Committee he entirely objected to the bracketing together of , Warrington and•Widnes as being in oppotition to the. Ship Canal Bill. :He would proceed to show how un- fair the statement was. ihe agreement between the Corporation and the company was practically decided ,1 by bath parties long before the Tidal ,Openings •Bill of the company was-oven prejezted, and that agreement' being: completed and practically out of tleeoway they addressed themselves to the consideration of the pos- sible effect of the Tidal Openings Bill. They ditl present a petition against the Bill in the House of Commons. They were obliged to do so by a certain ,date to obtain the necemary /oozes .standi, but having securechit they applied themselves to the consideration of the probable effects of the Bill, and they took into counsel Mr. Wolfe Larry, their standing adviser on matters affecting the river, and also Mr. Carter Bell in regard to the qaestion of sanitation. Both these gentlemen advised them, in terms which the Council had had before-them, that they could consistently support the Ship Canal and the ,Tornn Clerk presented a petition in favour of the Bill before the Commons' Committee, and that was sup- ported by Mr. 'Longdin and others, and the result was that the Commons' Committee accepted the Bill almost in its entirety, making only slight alterations, but not in principle- and it was a great misfortune that the Bill az passeciloy the Committee of the House of Com- mons had not been passed in exactly the same way by the Lords. After the Bill left the Comrnons',Committee and came before the Lords' Committee,the Borough Stir- veyor, the Town Clerk and Mr. Wolfe Barry, gave the most strenuous support to the Ship Canal gill. All througkwith the exception of a purely technical opposi- tion, the Corporation had cordially and thoroughly, and at considerable expense, supported the Ship Canal Com- pany, and they had given them in the person of Mr. Wolfe Barry the most valuable piece of evidence in sup- port of their contention for the retardation of the water that could -have been obtained, and the result was that the Committee had granted that portion of the Bill which referred to the closing of the tidal openings, but they refused the ether important element in the Bill which bad reference to the retardation The evidence called by the Mersey Docks and Harbour Board—given by Lord iKelvin and their engineer.showed that retardation at a certain period was necessary and desirable,and could not inure anybody. The only differ- ence between the opponents and promoters of the Bill was the question of time that the retardation should be allowed to go along—whether it should be three hours and forty minutes sought by the objectors.or four hours and thirty minutes claimed by the promoters. Thus the only difference with reference to this questien was the email one of 50 minutes. Yet in face of this, the Lords had refused to allow any retardation whatever. He had heard people say, "Thank God for the House of Lords." it never was an opinion cf his. (Laughter.) The MAYOR No politics, please. (Laugbter.) Councillor ROBERTS, continuing, said that his early training and experience had confirmed his opinion as to the intellectual calibre of the noble Lords who eat there. (Renewed laughter.) Alderman SUTTON: It is all very well." Councillor ROBERTS proceeding, remarked that he re- gretted the decision the more because Alderman Fran- comb and others haa repeatedly called attention to the insanitary condition of a section of the canal and river. Their main object in assisting the Ship Canal Company with their Bill was two-fold. Fir-tly, to improve the sanitary condition of the water, and secondly„by adding to the scour to improve the condition of the channel and maintain as far as possible the Bft. channel to which they were entitled by the agreement. , What made it harder was that the very rater which was now bound to go out again on the immediate com- mencement of the ebb of the tide was the water that had , been taken out of their river by the construction of the Ship Canal, and, which if anybody was entitled to it, it was themselves. It seemed to him that the decision could not be allowed to stop where it was any longer than was absolutely necessary. They could do nothing</p>
        <p>A. A. ZIMMERMANN, ESQ., CHAMPIONCYCLIST OF TEM WORLD.—" I think Kops Ale very adapts ble for athletes,and during my visit at Liverpool have found it very refreshing lfter bard work." FRED. MARTIN, ESQ., AMATEUR HOLDER OF LONDON TO BIT 3 AND BACA RECORD, &amp;c., says :—" I have a high ap- preciation of Hops Ale, and have drank noth'ng else in all my long distance and record rides for the past three years. It is the cyclists' drink. Have tried every other beverage but prefer Hops." Fifteen Gold Medals awarded. Two million bottles sold in one week. Bittered from choicest Hops only. Agent :—W. FURNESS, Hop Stores, Bewsey-at., Warrington,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>' MUSED/C.</head>
        <p>' MUSED/C.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS, in moving the 'minutes artbe Museum, &amp;c., Committee, referred to the addi, ion tothe 41uselim made by the= specimens received from The 'British Museum and the further gift of 181 specimens of 'reptiles and batrachian3 made by Mr. Linnaeus eresn. and said that they' had now one ot the finest collec- tions outside the Britith Museum. Heinvited members of the Council and their friends to visit the Museum-and other departments unCer the charge of'the c )rnmitteeota .they were anxious tottimulate pubhp'interest in one of the most valuable public institutions rm the town. (ear, hear.) Councillor FURNESS seconded, and the minutes-mare adopted.</p>
        <p>THE GAS COMMITTEE -THE QUESTION OF ADVERTISING.-gTRONG SPEECH BY COM- CILLOR ROBERFE.</p>
        <p>Councillor HEPSIDED moved the ,confirmation of the minutes of the Gas Committse. ''Heezemarked that they had been able to place a tender for ?nearly £4OO with a Warrington firm, namely the tender of the Pearson and :Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., to supply a wrought iron roof for the purifier house at Longford at £394. Councillorlromense asked in what paper or papers the following tender had been advertised for, namely, the tende of Mr.Jopenhldorten to supply 20,000 arch bricks at 625. 6d.per I,ooo.and the tender of Messrs.Haroer and Moore to at pply 5000 square bricks, 3-inch, at66s. 9d. per 1,000, and the tender of Messrs. Trotter, Hainec9 and Corbett to sunplie:6o,ooo arch and square bricks at-535. per 1,000. Had it been advertised for in the local .2apers Councillor HYMNED said it had been advertisedier in ,the "Gas Journal." Councillor Funteess : Is that the only paper'? Councillor FIEFHERD : So far att I know. Councillor ROBERTS then moved as.an amendment that the minutes of the Gas Committee be confirmed 'with the exception of the tender referred to. He pointed out that a resolution was passed by the • Councilsin 1887 to the effect that any tender involving ars expenditure of £2O or upwards, shall NI publicly advertised forcand that every accepted tender should be published with the minutes of the committee conneeted therewithetogether with the price and the name of theue.ccessfelfeem. The otiect of that resolution was, he said, two WA. One was to procure peebiloity in regard to the expenditure of money on contract account by the Corporation; the second and principal object was to give the-Ratepayers of Warrington who found the 'money, an eppor., tutity at any rate to participate in that ex- penditure by having a chance of -tendering. Every other committee of the Corporation with the exception of the Gas Committee had interpreted that resolution in the,cnly way it was intended to be interpreted by ad- vert:sing in the local papers. What is the use of adver- tisine for the requirements of that Corporation in a newnpaper which did not circulate half-a-dozen copies in the borough of Warrington-1 Must they take that particular newspaper which it suited .certain officials of the Corporation to advertise their requirements irrß He at once exonerated the:Gas. Committee for participation in that conduct. He Vl.BO glad the gaseagineer was pre- sent because he intended to fix the responsibility for this actiensupon him. .It would not do for any official of that Corporation to so deliberately misinterpret the spiritof a resolution of that kind—to comply with the letter of it, but to defeat its object by advertising in a. paper which only circulated three or four copies a week in the borough. He thought neanember of the Council would refuse tceadmit that the, ratepayers of Warrington had at least an e,qual right—he would not say a greater right —to have an opportunity for tendering for the require- ments of-that Corporationrin any department whatever, whether it was a matter oftlron, bricks or clothing, or ewhatever else it might be. ,Cut ever since Mer. Haddock 'became manager of the gas works that -resolution had 'teen stultifked time after time. He did not wish to press the amendment. .He.rose,principally for the purpose of getting theiprinciple emphasised that' the people of War- rineton—those who found the,money should have some .voice in selecting the tune. if that resolution needed to be.made,clear tothe gas engineer—be did notthink any- one else needed to have it made clear to them—he was prepared to move that the resolution be altered so as to say that tenders be advertised in the local papers. ,Every other committee had loyally and faithfully observed that resolution. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor FTIENESS seconded the amendment. He did not think as councillors they would be doing their duty if they.allowed tenders to go by default out of the town for the simple reason that an opportunity was not given to local manufactcrers to tender. Alderman SUTTON thought that in justice to the gas engineer they should not allow it to go forth to the public that he was entirely to blame. Ile might have acted on ,the instructions of the committee. Very few CoeporatioEs had tenders for everything they wanted. The Paving and Sewerage Committee for instance, did not advertise:; they went direct to the quarries, and the Gas Committee would probably be better served by the parties inentioned in th,e tender than anyone else. Councillor RCEIERTS : Nonsense. , Alderman SurroN : I think the committee have acted within their rights, and in accordance with the resolu- tion in adefertising in the papers they have done. Alderman BOLTON I don't think Mr. Haddock should with all this. The committee is quite large b e charged c enoughhaaendd influential enough as any of the Corporation in the matter of pounds, shillings and pence, and to to make the best bargain when toey have an opportunity. Councillor POBERTS 1 You say Mr. Haddock io to blame. That is not the point. Alderman Bevrox : I take the responsibility upon myself as a member of the Gas Committee. It has been the fault of the local manufacturers in not seeing the Gas Journal." Councillor ROBERTS: Who takes the "Gas Journal" in Warrington ? Alderman BOLTON: I have nothing to do with that. The Standing Orders have been complied with. At the Same time, I admit that the local papers ought to have the first chance, hut we have sometimes to tender to a</p>
