      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>so far as this session was concerned, but next session, if the Ship Canal Company came before Parliament for powers to obtain the retardation, it would be their bounden duty on the important grounds of the sanitary condition of the water, and the hardly less important condition of the channel itself, that they should take action in the matter with that object. Alderman BOLTON seconded the adoption of the minutes. Re said that the condition of that portion of the river that had been blocked up at one end opposite St. James's Church was in a most scandalous condition, and if it could not be flushed sooner or later, there would be an epidemic of typhoid fever. The Ship Canal was gett- ing worse every year. The River Preservation Com- missioners and the River Pollution Committee were stopping all minerals, dyes, and chemicals that used to act as disinfectants, from being emptied into it, with the result that nothing was getting into the Canal ex- cept sewage. There was no chance of disinfectants getting into the river. An epidemic was only a question of time. The canal was getting worse year by year. Councillor Saaw-GREEN said the decision of the Lords Lad given a very great deal of concern to many people in the town. He regretted that Councillor Roberts did not proceed a little further, and tell them what diffr- ence it would make. Would there come no water at all over Walton sluices? Would there be nothing but stag- nant water in that part? Councillor ROBERTS said that while the matter was most unsatisfactory because they had not got what they wanted and were entitled to, it was not quite as bad, perhaps, as that statement of fact alone would lead one to suppose. They had obtained by clauses joint control over the Walton sluices and therefore they would be able at certain tides to divert the water on its flow into the river—on all tides 16ft. and upwards. There were 290 sixteen feet tides annually that water coule be got thratzgh at Walton sluices on the flood tide, or equal to 24 per month. The MAYOR In additien to Butcher's Field? Councillor Ronmers : Yes, in addition to the improve- ment at Butcher's Field. By the agreement also, the Ship Canal Company were bound to construct a pen- stock at the end of the old river through which the tide rising in the Ship Canal would f_nd its way down the old river and into the river diversion. What he had said would tend to re-assure their minds that while they had mot got the whole of the loaf they had a considerable .slice, and on the principle of pegging away he believed they would not be long before they got all they required. .(Hear, bear.) The minutes were then passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE ELECTRIC LIGHT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE ELECTRIC LIGHT.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved the adoption of the minutes of the General Purpos-s Committee, one of the resolutions of which was to the effect that the Town Clerk be in- structed to oppose an application for power to supply electrical euergy to Warrington which an electric light- ing company intended to make to the Board of Trade. Alderman FRANCOMRE seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor Barilearr thought the opposition to the elec- tric lighting company was only justified on the assump- tion that the Corporation intended to pravide electrical facilities themselves. One or two local tradesmen have gone so far as to instal electricity in their own prenaises. Councillor ROBERTS said it was not the intention of the General Purposes Committee to allow any outside authority to come there and obtain powers for providing the electric light which the Corporation would after- wards have to pay at an enormously increased value. They had had too much buying of monopolies and to add another one of the most important and valuable nature to the rest would 'be absurd in the extreme. It was merely with the idea of preserving this right they were opposing this outside—this foreign—cempany from coming to acquire rights which really belonged to the people of Warrington. Councillor lEirerrorr said the Gas Committee were, if they had applications from a certain number of the inhabitants of Warrington, prepared to deal with the electric light. If Mr. Bennett and half-a-dozen other people with some substance about them would apply they would be able to put them in the light. Alderman SrrroN thought Conncillor Heaton was quite rlght in saying that the Gas Committee would be pre- pared to undertake to put in the electric light if they saw the necessity for it. At present he was quite satisfied it would not pay to proceed. The minutes were then con'firmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>QUESTION OF RECREATION GROUNDS.</head>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF RECREATION GROUNDS.</p>
        <p>Councillor ELLisoN moved the following resolution of which he had given notice :—That it is desir- able the Trown Council should take into con- sideration the subject of providing a public recreation ground for the Latchford Ward." He pointed out, in the first place, that the resolution did not commit the Council to any special position except that it was desirable to consider the matter. He be- lived thatthe most successfel local government was that whose aim and object was to secure the health, happi- ness, prosperity, and contentment of the people. It was with this object in view, coupled with the legitimate demands ct modern life for open spaces, that land should be set apart for the physical exercise and recreation of the workieg classes. Latchford was becoming one of the most important wards in their borough, and to all appearances it would go on increasing. He supposed that it contained over 15,(100 inhabitants and 900 voters and before many years brad gone all the available land wocld be tahen up for building purposes. That beinc the case, they, the residents of Latchford, thought that a sufficient quantity of land should be secured and laid out for the exercise and enjoyment of the inhabitants. Perhaps i- might be said that suffi- cient provlsion was already made for the whole of the borough in their Town Hall, Park and Gardens. They must however take into consideration the fact that Latchford however was somewhat isolated from 'other parts of,the borough. They should alse consider the dis- tance from the upper part of Latchford. If a working man went to pay a visit tc the Borough Park, he would have two miles to walk and another two miles back,mak- ing a four miles visit. He might go outs show the need of a place for the recreation of their children and young people, bat he thought it quite unnecessary. He had heard it said that they should have secured S. James' Vicarage for the purpose nut he did not think that was sufficiently large. I Alderman Surrox seccnded the motion. He thought lit was desirable that they should have recreation grounc I, not only in Latchford but in every other part of the town as well. As Councillor Ellison represented Latchford ke (Llderman Sutton) thought he might try to rilnd some patriotic individual to give some ground to answerthe purpose. The Mazes : Let some one buy the Old Warps. (Laughter.) Councillor LEE supported the motion. _</p>
        <p>Councillor TenozioN also supported the motion. He thought it very desirable indeed to have recreation grounds in the borough, and he h(Nped the Corporation would take into consideration the desirability of furnish- ing cecreation grounds not osly for Latchford but for the other wards as well. He suggested that the Corpora- tion might rent the piece of ground near the old bar racks from Mr. John White.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said Mr. White had promised to furnish them witn plans of land that he thought would be suit- able for open spaces all over Vise borough where las had control of the land.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said they had had offered to them the old cemetery in Latchford, bat it was upon condi- tions with which they could not comply. The MLTOR said they all agreed that Latchford should have a proper recreation ground. The motioa was then carried, ad the meeting ter- minated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LONDON TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>A LONDON TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>A NOTICE TO QUIT FOLLOWED BY A DOUBLE</p>
        <p>MURDER.</p>
        <p>About half-past ten on Saturday night a terrible tragedy was enacted in North-st., Caledonian-road,Lon- dote, where a butcher named Eiward Galley has for , some time carried on business as a working-man's but- cher. His landlord and landlady, Mr. and Mrs. Riley. who carried ea the business of sanitary plumbers and engineers, lived next door. The shop occupied by Galley was held at a weekly rental, and last week Mr. Riley gave him notice to quit. Galley was said to bs doing an excellent business, and this acti n on the part of the landlord greatly annoyed him. He was heard to vow vengeance against Mr. Riley for what he chose to con- sider an attempt to ruin him. At eight o'clock on Saturday night Galley saw Riley and asked if the notice held good, and, receiving an affirmative reply, said he would be revenged. From time to time he took drink at a neighbouring tavern, and at alf-past ten he en- tered Riley's kitchen, where the husband and wife were at supper. With a knife from his block Galley inflicted , fearful wounds op the back of Riley's neck, severing the 1 arteries and the spine. Mrs. Riley who attempted to escape, was also instantly killed by terrible wounds on the neck. Galley was arrested. The Press Association says that Galley has made a long statement to the police about the crime. In it he says lie owed Riley only a week's rent. Riley asked him for it, and at the s!Line time gave him a written notice to deliver up possession of his premises on July 11. After that Galley says he gave up serving customers and worked himself into a terrible passion, which culmin- ated in his going to the back door of the premises occu- pied by the Rileys. " What's this ?" said Mr. Riley. I said " What it is," and "I did it," added the prisoner in an offhand and careless manner. Galley further said that he had taken the shop when it was empty and had worked the business until he was takine fifty pounds a week. He seemed to think that Riley was giving him notice in order to let the shop on better terms owing to the business that was now being done there,and he says that it vas" rough" on him to receive a week's notice after working up the business as he had done.</p>
        <p>To MOTHERS.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers fu their children while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poo; sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes 'al bright as a button." Of all chemists,:ls. I.d. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>"The monster was about 90ft. long. His head re- sembled that of a serpent, except that the mouth ap- peared more ferocious, the neck was narrow, and the body exactly like that of any other serpent, the colour being of a dingy, dirty hue. The serpent was carrying his bead some 14ft. or 15ft. out of the water, this being moved rapidly from side to side and backward and for- ward, somewhat similar to the way a swan moves its head and neck. As far as could be seen ,the ophidian had no flappers, and it appeared to go through the water solely by the means of unceasing undulations of the body, which was humped into three distiact arches."</p>
        <p>All this was seen, says the "Cambria Daily Leader, by a "jolly salt" of Swansea. But why so early? July is young; and yet we have the sea-serpent. He has come, like early peas or forced strawberries, and, under such conditions, he (like these delicacies) seems to want something of his natural flavour. The " humps" are promising, as far as they go, but we expected something far mote lurid from the 'jolly salt" of Swansea.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frederick Nicholls Crouch, the composer of I "Kathleen Mavourneen," is still living at Baltimore, U.S.A., a recent report of his death being wholly bace- less. Mr. Crouch, who is now ia his eighty-ninth year, has just written to his nephew, an architect at Liver- pool, a letter, dated the 20'zti ult., in which be says : "The newspapers have announced my death and burial so often that they will have to resort to an affidavit (25c.) to make their swindle appear feasible even in sen- sational prints. When the old bard is really about to die he will write his own obituary. I went to bear my 'Green and Gold" played by a military orchestra yes- terday, and lam to conduct the orchestra on Monday night and sing (at eighty-nine) Kathleen Mavourneen ' in public. Proof positive this that your uncle lives. How I shall acquit myself the result will show. In mental spirits lam as bright as ever, but physically I am worn out. My two daughters appear in the same performance for the whole week. . . . Through all my sickness, I have adhered to my practice of writing a specific article, music, prose, or poetry, every day, and perfecting the same."</p>
        <p>The statistician has at last got down to the startling I and powerful influence of the bicycle on the trade of the country. He finds that in fifteen industrial branches which have beenirpost affected hv'the cycling fever there is now a yearly loss of nearly £30,000,000. Those who make a business of catering to the wants of norse riders and drivers have suffered to the extent of £9,000,000 annually, "horses and livery" showing a deficit of £4,000,000 "pleasure vehicles" £3,000,000,and "harness and saddles " £2,000,000. The bicycle is responsible for a falling off of £2,700.000 in piano sales, £1,600,000 in jewellery, and £BOO,OOO in watches. The consumption of cigars has dropped by £1,600,000, and of confection- ery by £1,200,000, while tailors mourn a loss la £2,000,000, and the purveyors of dress goods and millinery a similar amount. The omnibuses and sub- urban railways are estimated to have suffered to the extent of £600,000 annually, but in all probability this is an absurdly low calculation. Among the remaining trades, the sellers of whisky and beer are shown to have lost £500,000, railroads, excursions, etc., £500,000, and theatres, £400,000.</p>
        <p>'lbis is a very thorough all-round indictment for the bicycle, but it is likely that it tells little more than half the story of the social revolution which the wheel is working. For histance, in the foregoing hat nothing is said of the inroads that must inevitably be made on the aggregate income of the medical profession. An eminent physician is quoted as saying that "not within 200 years has there been any one thing which has so benefited mankind as the invention of the bicycle," and "that hundreds of thousands of men and women who for- merly practically had no exercise are now devoting half their time to healthy recreation, and not only streng- thening and develo•Ang their own bodies, but are pre-. paring the way for future generations, which will be born of healthy parents." We have been a nation of dyspeptics simply because we did not take enough phy- sical exercise to develop and strengthen our bodies. The bicycle will put all this right, and it is safe to say that the loss in " drugs " and " doctor's fees will be far too significent an item to be omitted from future statis- tics on the commercial influence of the new national pastime.</p>
        <p>So marvellous were the arithmetical feats of Herr Heinhaus at the Westminster Aquarium on Tuesday that one might almost be pardoned for ascribing them to magic rather than to mathematical skill. To this pecu- liarly gifted man figures present absolutely no difficulty. He is a living interest-table, and is able to tell the value of anything under £lOO in German marks almost before the question is propounded. His speed is solvice: doubts as to the day of the week on which September 5, 1838, fell was quite disconcerting; while on hearing the date of a man's birthday he instantly told him hew many seconds he had lived. This "lightning calculator," who surpasses all mere reckoning machines in promptitute of solution will doubtlees hold his place in the Aquarium entertainment for some time to come. For those people who like surprises, his " turn " will provide a plentiful feast. Herr Heinhaus was formerly head bookkeeper in a German mercantile house.</p>
        <p>One of the most objectionable forms of relief given to landownerS out of the rates is the employment of the rural police, paid and maintained by the public,aa game- keepers. The question has been raised upon the Norfolk County Council, and referred to the responsible commit- tee for consideration. The scandal is a general one in game preserving districts throughout the country. The police are taken from their proper duties in order to save rich landowners the expense of employing a few more keepers, the county police committees that sanction the arrangement generally consisting of the class that bene- fits by it. The farmer, who sees his crops damaged by the game that he three not touch, is called upon, as a ratepayer, to pay the bill for protecting the destructive creatures. It is high time th it some county should take the lead in breaking down tt is system of game preserv- ing at the public expense.,</p>
        <p>A striking example of English as she is sometimes written abroad is furnished in the bill of fare of a certain Italian railway buffet. During the" 55 minits of stops " at this station the English traveller is invited to par- take of a" Porridge " (for some unexplained reason this is the translation offered for menu), which starts with the promising item " Slender at the Saint-Ger- main" (Postage Saint-Germain). Next comes "Pike forced meat ball, of the Cenis-Mount " with " Sauce of the Financial," followed by " Beef to bake, with Pota- toes-Pea-Vegetable." The next dish masquerades under the fascinating title of "Poultry Snow-drop of the Breese at the Jelly." The French menu styles it " Galantine de Volaille de Breese ri, la Gel."" Salad of the season" accompanies this delicacy, and to conclude we have " Sidedish and stocked Dessert." " Stocked "is good, but the French gives " Dessert assorti." With such a " Porridge" before him the travelling Englishman should truly feel at home, and the price is only 31r. (wine included.)</p>
        <p>For the second time an American driving meet in Hyde Park was made a feature of the celebra- tion of Independence-day. But it cannot be said that the assemblage of vehicles at the Magazine on Saturday morning was at all imposing. It was, however, most eccentric. The skeleton-like charac- ter of the carriages—nearly all of them two- wheeled, and drawn by single horses—was their main characteristic. Of beauty in form there was little, everything being sacrificed in most instances to considerations of speed. Twenty-four whips attended the meet—well-known people all of them in the Anglo-American world. The strangest-look- ing vehicle of all was a" Michigan bike," driven by Mr. W. Winans. At one o'clock the somewhat motley procession made its way round the park, attracting a certain measure of curiosity,but nothing approaching the interest usually excited by the old- established coaching meets.</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL. LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the week ending July 3rd, 166 seagoing cargo ves:els, with the aggregate net registered tonnage of 50,788 tons, arrived at and sailed from the port of Man- chester, against 177 ship a of 52.323 tons in the preceding week. Of these 42 vessels el 25.975 tons entered from or cleared for foreign perts,and 166 vessels of 24,813 tone arrived or sailed coastwise. Seven vessels arrived with timber and wood pulp from Canada, the Baltic, and the White Sea, three with logwood from the West ladies, two with flints from St. Valery, one with bane ash from Ria Grande.one with wheat from San Francisco,and one with esparto grass from Almeria, whilst miscellaneous cargoes were imported from Antwerp. Rotterdain,'Flan,- burg, Boulogne, Copenhagen, Trenort, Rouen, Paris, Bordeaux, Nantes, Leghorn, Naples, and Sicily. Generr.k cargoes were cleared outwards to Bahia and Rio do Janeiro, the Persian Gulf, St. Petersburg, Riga and Revel, AlexanAria and Syria, Belgium, Holland, Den- mark, Sweden, Hamburg, Rouen, Paris, Mazarron and Bordeaux. There were also sailings to Maranhana and Quebec. A large amount ef cargo was loaded anl dis- charged by the regular steamers running to and from the home ports, whilst 84- head of cattle and 196 sheep were imported from nublin.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Wilson, the wife of a betting-man named George Wilson, of Abyssinia-road, Clapham Junction, met a terrible death at Clapham Junction on Saturday night It is stated that Mrs. Wilson and her husband, who had beer: separated for some years, met on the platform at the Junction on Saturday evening at about six o'clock. They left together, and at nine o'clock came back to the platform,where an altercation took place, during which, it i, stated, Mrs. ‘Vilson took hold of her husband's coat. In freeing himself from her grasp he swung her around, accidentally throwing her on the line, and her body was cut in two by a goods train which at that moment passed through the statirren. Wilson was arrested.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL TEACHER IN DANGER.</p>
        <p>A Sunday school teacher, Miss Octavia Bacon, of Lincoln Uouso, Wykeham-street, Strood, has (says the "Chatham News ") been in serious peril. Questioned in regard to it, Miss Bacon said she was a teacher in St. Nicholas Church Sunday School. "Until some three months ago, I had hardly known for five years," she said, " what it was to he well. I suffered very badly. I was positively afraid to eat any food at night-time, because I could not get any rest: I used tio sit up half the night, and walk about the room in awful pain. I could scarcely get my breath, and felt that I should choke."</p>
        <p>"My other daughter," interpolated her mother, " went into a decline just the same, and died." Miss Bacon added—" I was very low-spirited and melancholy. I couldn't bear anybody to speak to me. I used to sit and cry all day. I had no appetite what- ever. I wished I could die. I was attended by quite half-a-dozen doctors, and at St. Bartholomew's Hospital, in London. All the doctors told me that I was consump- tive. Nothing that I took effected any change until " Until what ? " queried the reporter. " Until I tried Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People," replied Miss Bacon. "What induced you to try them, after everything else had failed?"</p>
        <p>" Well, sir, three months ago one of the workmen at Messrs. Tiagey's cement works on the Medway told my father of the great benefit his daughter had derived from them so I went and bought a box at once. Believe me sir, I felt great relief before the first box was gone. Since I began to take them, I have not suffered at al/ from indigestion or otherwise. My friends remark up,-11 the change in my appearance, and say how fat and well lam looking. My appetite has returned, and I eat well, sleep regularly, and can attend to my duties as &amp;- teacher." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills curo not only cases; I like the above, but all the many disorders which arise</p>
        <p>from an impoverished state of the blood and nerves,such as acreinia, pale and sallow complexion, weakness, loss of appetite, shortness of breath, pains in the back nervous headache, early decay,all forms of female weak— ness, hysteria, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, sciatica, pal- pitations, low vitality, and wasting strength from any cause. These Pills are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by chemists and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46. Holborn-viaduct, London, E.G., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 133. 9d. They are unrivalled as a tonic and strength- ening medicine for both sexes.</p>
