      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>COAL TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE.</p>
        <p>The Executive of the Miners' Federation of Great' Britain met in Lon Jon on Tuesday to receive reports and consider the respective proposals of the owners and the miners with regard to the continuance of the Concilia- tion Board and the rate of wages. The various districts' within the Federation have pronounced with practical' unanimity against the proposal of the employers that a reduction in wages should be submitted to. At the meet on Tuesday 327,000 miners were represented. The pro— ceedings were conducted in strict privacy. An informal meeting of the Coal Trade Conciliation Board was held at Westminster on 'Wednesday. Accord- ing to the official report the coalowirsrs proposed ,that the Board should be continued from the Ist of August to determine the rate of wages from time to time. On behalf of the men it was proposed that the Bard should be continued for the next two years and that wages should remain as they are for the next 17 months, but that in the last seven months_ of the period the men should be at liberty to ask foe'an advance. To this the coalowners refused to accede, and the Conciliation Board is regarded as practically at an end.</p>
        <p>The ttoyal Laund-esses say Critchley's " STARCH GLOSS Is uneq,..alled for imparting a beautiful finish to starched inen Sm., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr.—. Corn- wall. says :--" My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss.' From Mrs.—, Bradford :—Dear sir,— I am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be übed in every house. Sold at ld., 301., and 6d., by most starch sellers. If not in stock witl procure it. T. Cum aLEY, Blackburn. Proprietor.—Advt.</p>
