        <p>REMUNERATIVE PROFIT.</p>
        <p>A.</p>
        <p>eric.an rile An • Ix can hardly be expected that I E nglishmennglishmen will read with any satisfaction the proceedings in ec,onection with the nominations for the American Presidency. Both of the great parties have now declared their policy, and in neither case is any favour shown to this country. The R3publicans have selected llr. MCKINLEY as their candidate !or presidency, and the chief plank in their plat- form is that high protection with which the name of Alr. MCKINLEY is identified. The barrier of Protection it is needless to say, is raised chiefly for the purpose of keeping British manufactured Pods out of the United States. The Democratic Ita,,artY have not yet fixed upon their candidate for 40 presidency, but they have framed a pro- The most important—in fact the '"iolportant—plank is to be found in the Pr°Posal to make silver, at a ratio of six- to one with gold, legal tender for the payment of debts in the United States. Here, !Pio, the British—or at all events, that in- Inlential section of them whose headquarters are PoPularly supposed to be in Lombard-street, L. oadon—are largely aimed at. The Democrats for the most part hail from the Western and the Southern States and it is roundly asserted that tile West and the South are in the hands of the :rhdon and New York money-lenders. Accord- 1g to one speaker at the Democratic Convention toverYthing is mortgaged in the West—from farms sewing machines. The policy of the Demo- ir.ats Will make the payment of these debts easier. %Silver be declared legal tender: at a ratio of B,l',en to one silver of the intrinsic value of £3 'll go as far as £5 in gold at the present time. Tlxis Proposal is of course denounced by the oldites" as sheer confiscation, and it Ler- t4l4lY amounts to partial repudiation of debts. A reason for the tremendous develop- -141 e4t of the agitation for bi-metallism is not 17 to seek. The farmers of the Western States 044 been heavily hit by the appreciation of gold. wing to the rapid decline of prices they are new 'robably under the necessity of selling fifty per 'tetk_lit• more produce in order to meet their debts '44 4 Was the case a few years ago, and all this of trrse makes it more difficult for them to meet ve claims of the money-lenders. It seems to be C,elt for granted that the Democrats will be ,elesaly beaten by the Republicans in the con- for the presidency, but that remains to be **tit 4. There are many signs to indicate that the 21Iggle will be keenly and even bitterly fought, 4.411(1 such strong feeling is being aroused that some atillerioan writers even go so far as to compare the .Itte of affairs with that which prevailed at the ; _eeiclential election which immediately preceded me Civil War of thirty odd years ago.</p>
        <p>SANKEY-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PARK MACHINE BAKERY,</p>
        <p>PE riBAKtRY is fisted up on the most improved HY_ eitie Principles in Ovens and Machinery with a health and oomf view to the cleanliness of the goods produced and the Ott of the workmen.</p>
        <p>BREAD --u ..a..,READ AND CONFECTIONERY,</p>
        <p>11" IS BREAD AND BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>AWARD AND DIPLOMA</p>
        <p>PURITY, SWEETNESS AND EXCELLENCE.</p>
        <p>SAMUEL GEORGE DAVEY, +-ATE DIRRCTOR AND SECRETARY OF "NRY MILLING IN]) CO. LIMITED, S PURCHASED THE BUSINESS FOUNDED ny 111 MLA, B. A. SMITH at AND 4 A ADEMY-ST., AND DUTTERMARKET - ST.,</p>
        <p>ARRINGTON. AND lIAS ODDE THE PREMISES wrr vitEz,-.11 OICE SELECTION OF THE Wu FINEST QUALITY GOODS. OFFERED AT THE LOWEST</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SI7CCESS OF A RUNCORN CYCLIST.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Johnson, of Runcorn, recorded another to the long list of successes by carrying off the second prize in the two miles cycle handicap at Eccles Oddfellows Sports, on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>RUNCORN TRADE IMPROVEMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TRADE IMPROVEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee held a meeting this (Friday) morning in the Town Hall Runcorn,and Mr. Wilding the surveyor read a report which he had been instrdcted to prepare on the land and buildings available in the town adopted to the purpose of manufactures. ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The teachers and choir in connection with the above church took their annual outing on Wednesday, Rtarting at eight o'clock on that morning for Kelsall by waggon- ettes. That resort was duly reached and a very pleasant day was spent in the locality. Dinner and tea was served, being prepared in a satisfactory manaer by Mrs. Wright. The riarty a. rived back aboutlo o'clock.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION AT RUNCORN. Constable Thomas Butler was presented this week with a handsome tea service, the gift of the Runcorn Petty Sessional Police Force on the occasion of his marriage with Miss %Valley, a daughter of EK-Sergeant Thomas %Valley, now residing at New Brighton. P.C. Bntler has been connected with the police force in Run- corn for about two years. VOLUNTEERS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The members of the Runcorn Volunteers join the 2nd (Earl of Chesters) Volunteer Battalion Chester Regiment to which they belong, and will spend nest week under canvass at Towyn. The district is pleasantly situated for the purpose and as capital arrangements have been made, no doubt ttie week's change will be beneficial.</p>
        <p>GIFT OF BOOKS TO THE SEADIENS' INSTITUTE, RUNCORN. Miss H. Hayes, of Frodsham has presented the Run- corn Branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen with 18 handsomly-bound and popular literary works for the use of the seamen using the Institute. The gift has been greatly appreciated both by the habitues of the Institute and Mr. Shaw and his committee.</p>
        <p>THUNDERSTORM AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A severe thunderstorm broke over Runcorn on Thurs• day evening. For about two hours rain fell incessantly accompanied by vivid lightening fl tshes and heavy claps of thunder. No damage or accident was reported. Several sewers in the town were blocked up and the streets for sometime were flooded by the unusual rain- fall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>COLLISION ON TRH SHIP CANAL AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>COLLISION ON TRH SHIP CANAL AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the dredger Alpha collided with the Queen of the Mersey nearly opposite the Bridgewater Locks. Runcorn. The Alpha was travelling outwards, while the Queen of the Mersey was moored to the wall. The Alpha was injured in the plates, and the Queen of the Mersey had her paddlebox smashed. There was a fog prsvailing at the time.</p>
        <p>THE TIMBER TRADE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Durieg the week end the third Norwegian barge con- taining timber arrived at the Runcorn lay by. It con- tained nearly 1,000 loads of timber which will be carried by lighters to Manchester. There has been during the week three vessels at thee Weston Point hy by. The Hermon arrived on Friday with some 1,000 loads of timber and the Agatha with timber and the Nordby set carrying grain have bean discharging through the week. Several other vessels are expected with timber during the next week or two. RUNCORN CYCLING CLUBS.</p>
        <p>The Saturday section of the above club held an inter club run with the members of the Bough Green C.C. The members of the latter organisation were met near Thelwall but the weather was so bad that the run in- stead of being to Bowdon was curtailed and Warrington proved the stopping point. The Wednesday section again selected Chester which place was reached by a devious route embracing Liverpool and Birkenhead. The lengthy run was greatly enloyed and the weather, being everything to be desired, conduced to the pleasure ef the ride. BILLIARDS AT THE RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>During the last three months a billiard handicap has been in progress in this club and with good entries,good play has been the result. The two last left in to play off the final were Mr. H. Barnes and Mr. H. Farmer, well known as a member of the Runcorn football team. According to the handicap Farmer had 115 points to make against Runes 230 and he succeeded on Friday night in compiling that number while his opponent made 196. The runners up were Mr, R. Owen, third, and Mr. P. Marsh, fourth. The handicap of the players in the final tie was Farmer 15 behind, Barnes 130 behind in a game of 100 up. There were four prizes offered.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN UNION YEAR BOOK.</p>
        <p>Some useful intormatiou relative to the Rural District Council and the Runcorn Union is incorporated in the year book just published. In addition to the diary, the officials,tnembers of the different townshios,committees, pariah councils and parochial committees are clearly set forth. There is a table giving a list of salaries of the Union officials and the assistant overseers. The acreage of the Union is set down at 50,468, and the number of houses 8,520. The population in 14381 was 35,622, in 1891 44,713, and the rateable value taken to Lady Day, 1696—£242,128. The book has been compiled by Mr. J. Ashton, the clerk, and is another t€stimony to hie ability in dealing with all matters connected with the administration of the Poor Law. MARRIAGE OF A RUNCORN GRETLEMAN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last. at Georze-lane Congregational Church, Woodford, by the Rev. Thomas Hammond, Dr. William Rigby Fisher, son of Mr. W. Fisher, The Nook, Runcorn, was married to Miss Jane Curtis Littlejohn, eldest daughter of Thomas Littlejohn, of Woodford. The bride who was given away by her father, was attired in a drcss of ivory satin chiffonette, ornamented with orange blossom. She carried a shower bouquet of orchids and roses (the gift of the bridegroom), and was attended by the Misses Margaret and Clementina Littlejohn (sisters of the bride. Their gowns were composed of Japenese alpaca,with bodices of heliotrope satin and ehiffon, and hats to match. The bridesmaids wore bracelets and carried bouquets pre- sented by the bridegroom. The best man was Mr. Thomas P. Littlej )hn. The musical part of the service was well rendered by Miss Berrie Stephens, F.C.0., R.A.M., and a large congregation witnessed the ceremony. A reception was afterwards held at 4, The Cre cent, and later in the afternr4on the happy pair left for the East Coast, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was of nut brown cloth trim- med with silk, and Panama hat with feathers. There was a long list of handsome and valuable presents.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN</p>
        <p>The 96th anniversary in connection with the above schools was held on Sunday in the St. Paul's Wesleyan Chapel. Throughout the day good congregations at- tended to hear the admirable discourses of the Rev. H. May of Handsworth College. On Monday. the Rey. El. H. May gave his lecture entitled " Red white, bay grizzled and black horses,"before an appreciative assem- bly. The subject was allegorically treated and the characters viewed under the emblem of the horse. The stable was a man's heart wherein by the brushes of action be tended and groomed character. The lecturer endeavoured to show that as they passed into the 20th century greater stress was being laid upon character and moral worth. He drew anologies between the testing of character and the testing of horses and those who under- stood the technicalterm could apply the principles which he endeavoured to press home. He pointed out that men drove the horses of indifference, pride, immorality, passion fcr strong drink, laziness and selfishness. The lecturer concluded by referring to the white horse of holiness, the speaker urging all to drive this, for the armies of heaven followed Christ on white horses robed in linen white and clear. In the great judgment day character harnessed by holy deeds would be reviewed and crowned with an endless "Welt done." Special music was rendered at all the servizes by the choir and scholars conducted by Mr. J. Birkett, who it was evi- denced had bestowed great pains at the various re- hearsals. The chief piece was " The Rose of Sharon," the rendering of which wag very effective. The collec- tions amounted in all to £55 103.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>MEDALS FOR LANCASHIRE SOLDIERS</head>
        <p>MEDALS FOR LANCASHIRE SOLDIERS</p>
        <p>It is announced from the War Office that the Com- mander-in-Chief has awarded the Army medal for long service and good conduct to Colour-Sergeant B. Barrett, Quartermaster-Sergeant W. Costello, and Sergeant- Instructor of Musketry C. Howse. Royal Lancaster Regiment; Colour-Sergeant T. H. Sr.veeney, King's (Liverpool Regiment) ; Sorgeant P. Winters, Lancashire Fusiliers; Colour-Sergeant A. Steggles, North Lancashire Regiment; and Colour-Sergeant J. Howarth, Cheshire Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>SHOCKINO ACCIDENT AT A LEVEL</head>
        <p>SHOCKINO ACCIDENT AT A LEVEL</p>
        <p>C ROSSING,</p>
        <p>A shocking fatality is reported to have occurred on the railway near Graz, in Styria. A party of seven work- men and women from the cigarette factory at Gostin,g had (says the "Daily News" correspondent) spent the night in Graz dancinc, and drinking. At four o'clock on Wednesday morning they took a fiacre to return home. Five crowded inside, and two seated themselves with the coachman on the box. They attempted to cross the line Just. as the express from Trieste came along. Before the "Pine could be stopped the carriage had been pushed forwards on the line for a long distance. When the tram, was brought to a standstill it was seen that a heap of heeding humanity lay on the rail-. with one horse torn to pieces and the other plunging wildly. All those who were in the carriage had been killed. The coach- man was only slightly hurt, hut the two men who were with him aro seriously injured. The line, it appears, was not shut ofT from the road, the watchman having faUn asleep while reading.</p>
        <p>The Birmingham City C )uncil on Tuesday decided to lease the principal tramways of the city for 21 years to a Canadian syndicate, who undertake, amongst other things, to establish halfpenny fares.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday, under the presidency of Mr. J. Mclntyre, J.P., at the Workhouse, Dutton. There were also pre,!ent : Admiral Parry, Messrs. J. Weir, T. Wright, J. D trby- shire, C. Roylance, G. Pilling, H. E. Heywood, J. M. Williams, J. Brandreth, T.Taxter, C.C., W. Bradford,S. Woodward, and W. Pickering ; Mr. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Messrs. Dig&amp; and Swiwton (surveyors), Mr. Farringtol (nuisance inspector), and Dr. Adams (medical offi er of health).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER SATITROAY, JULY 11, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATITROAY, JULY 11, 1896.</p>
        <p>said estuary if the canal were not constructed, and in consequence the landowners who opposed the Bill there- fore withdrew from further opposition feeling that they had no longer any grounds, as the rejection of the clause which was BO far as they were concerned as effective as the throwing out of the Bill, but the District Council had other rights to protect. They had not only to see that they were protected from the effects of theiesewers Wile.b submerged, but also to provide for what might happen by the exclusion or partial exclusion of tidal waters from the canal, and as there did not appear to be any obligation on the Company to admit tidal water at all at Eastham and the exclusion or partial exclusion of tidal waters must tend to the waters of the canal becoming gradu- ally more pollu'..ed with the result that it might be that all sewage would sooner or later have to be clarified be- fore being allowed to 11w into the canal, sub-sections (3) and (4) of the clause granted are of the greatest importance. The Clerk in concluding his report said the committee who were appointed to assist him in settling the clauses obtained, had been of the greatest assistance, especially Mr. Heywood, wlio,as the Council knew, had been with him throughout, not only in the committee rooms of the House of Commons and the House of Lords but in any interview in Manchester or London with the representatives of the company and in consultation with the Parliamentary agents and counsel : and his suggestions and advice added to his arguments had gone a long way in securing the clauses granted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward proposed that the Board formally thank Mr. Heywood for the work he had done. He was sure Mr. Heywood had gone to a deal of trouble, and he thought the thanks of the Board were due to him.—Mr. Darbyshire seconded the resolution, and thought that Mr. Heywood apart from their thanks would think it a duty and a pleasure to do what he had done.—The motion was carried unanimously.— Mr. Heywood briefly responded and said he was indebted to them for their vote of thanks. What he had done,any member of the Council would have done with equal pleasure. It had been a useful experience to him. He had seen the operation of getting an act of parliament through the cammittees of the House of Commons an the House of Lords. It was something he had never seen before and he hoped that it would be useful,not only to him but the whole Council. He would like to say that Mr. Ashton laad been indeiatiguable in his endeav- ours and had thoroughly done all the duties required of I him. The visits Mr. Ashton had paid to Londou were very useful as Mr. Ashton had been able to get through other important worh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>SUTTON WATEft SUPPLY</head>
        <p>SUTTON WATEft SUPPLY</p>
        <p>The Parish Council of Sutton requested the District Council to write to the Local Government Board remind- ing them of their promise to hold an inquiry in Sutton re a watersupplv for the township. The Parish Council were very desirous that the enquiry should be held and the work done as expeditiously as possible. The Clerk said he understood the enquiry would be held shortly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PROTECTION OF LATCHFORD LOCKS.</head>
        <p>PROTECTION OF LATCHFORD LOCKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. J. Woodcock, clerk to the Grappenhall Parish Council, wrote to inform the District Council that the Ship Canal Company had not done anything in the way of protecting the Latchford ',lnks and to ask the coun- cil to write to the Company telling them that they wou/d be held reswinsible for any accident that might occur if the matter was nct attended to at once.—Mr. Weir Corn- plained of the delay of the Canal Company in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>DOCUMENTS OF THE HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>THE DOCUMENTS OF THE HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>_ The Northwich Ilural District Council forwarded auth- ority to the Council to apply for and receive all books, papers, and documents, relating to the business of the late Daresbury Highway Board.</p>
        <p>WESTON SEWERING</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Lake, law clerk to the Runcorn Urban Dis- trict Council wrote reminding the Council of his letter on the 25th March last, re the Weston sewers and call- ing attention to the fact that the three months therein mentioned for the Rural Council to make pro- posals within, had now lapsed and that no proposals had been submitted.—The Clerk said the Council had an agreement with the Urban District Council to turn the sewage of Weston into the Folly Brook on payment of £5. per year but the Ship Canal Company were under an agreement to get this right made perpetual, and Mr. Hunter the Ship Canal engineer, informed him that that had been done and he had replied to Mr. Lake to that effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</head>
        <p>PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Alfred D. A,trian, assistant secretary to The Local Government Board forwarded an order setting up as a temporary port sanitary authority for a period of three years a joint Board consisting of representatives of the town councils of Manchester and Salford and the Urb Council of Stretford. INIEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT. The Medical Officer reported that ..two cases of scarlet fever had occurred at Overton and Predsham Lordship, two cases at Stockton Heath, two at Aston and one at Moore. There had been no fresh cases of typhus fever at Halton. SIR G. GREENALL AND THE CANAL COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Henry Hunter, engineer to the Manchester Ship Canal Company forwarded the followiog letter from the agents to Sir Gilbert Greenell's estate,Lower Walton. —We have had a notice from the Parish Council for Sir Gilbert to clean out the Mill Pcnd. Hitherto this has been done by slushing it out into the brook and the cost has not been gteat. Possibly however.: you might not like us to adopt this course, new as, instead of going into the river as formerly the stuff would go into the Ship Canal. If it had to be carted away the total cost would be about £3O but we will adopt the latter method if you will pay one half on account of the Ship Canal."—Commenting upon this Mr. Hunter wrote.—" I beg particularly to draw your attention to the proposal made to turn the filth and sludge from Walton Mill Pool into the Ship Canal. lam objecting to this altogether and seeing the strong position that your Council ha i taken up lately as to the sanitary condition of the Canal, I will be obliged if you will'communicate with Messrs. White, Maclver and Co. and inform them that you as the sanitary authority obk.ct to this pollution. It is clearly Sir Gil- bert Greenall's duty to remove the filth from the pond, and it is unreasonable to expect the Ship Canal Com- pany to bear any part of an expenditure which properly belongs to him.—The Inspector was instructed to report on the matter.</p>
        <p>THE MERSEY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Potts, clerk to the Cheshire County Council, had written complaining of the offensive condition of the disused portion of the river Mersey at Warrington. and said that unless something was done to remedy it the nuisance would become very much worse as the summer advanced. He asked the District Council to take up the matter of the construction of the new sewers which they had arranged with the Corporation of Warrington, as early as possible, so that the sewers which caused the nuisance might be taken out of the old river bed and be no longer a danger to the public health. The attention of the Warrington Corporation having being called to this matter the Town Clerk replied by f wwarding copy of specifications, estimate and tenders for the construc- tion of the sewers for the approval of the District Coun- cil, and cn the recommendation of the Appleton Paro- ' chial Committee the spncifications, &amp;0., were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>REPORT FROM THE CLERK.</head>
        <p>A REPORT FROM THE CLERK.</p>
        <p>The Clerk presented a rejort with respect to the Man- chester Shin Canal Act 1896. He stated that the Bill had now hen passed by the committee of both houses of Parliament with the rosult that sq far as it affected the Rural District of Runcorn the- following clauses had been secured for the protection of the Council. "(1) Nothing in this Act contained shall prejudice,vary, diminish or affect the rights, powers, privileges and re- medies of the District Council, as the successors of the Guardians of the Poor of the Runcorn Union, under or by virtue of an agreement dated the fifth day of May one thousand eight hundred and ninety-one, and made between the said Guardians of the one part and the company of the other part, or relieve the company from any liabilities or obligations thereby imposed npon them, or render The District Council liable to any penalty or cost for doing any act or thing in accordance with the provisions of the said agreement. All the provisions of the said agreement' shall so far as applicable ap- ply to and in the case of all works to be executed by the company under the powers of this Act, and for that purpose such last mentioned works shall be deemed to be included within the expression. "The said canal works in the said agreements. (2). If by reason or in consquence of the construction of any of the works by this Act authorised, or the execution of any of the powers by this 'let conferred upon the company, or the non - fulfilment of any of the obligations by this Ant imposed upon the company, the level of the water in the canal of the company, or in the river Weaver, shall be so raised, or the ebb tide retarded, that the outfalla of any of the sewers, drains, brooks, or water courses, vested in or under the management, or control of the District Coun- cil, shall be submerged so such an extent as to render them, or any of:them appreciably less operative or effi- cient. or to cause them or any of them to be more fre- quently cleansed than they otherwise would have been or have required, the company shall execute all such works, and do all such things as shall be reasonably necessary for the proper discharge or disposal of the sewerage, or drainage, or cleansing to the satisfaction of the District Council. (3). If by reason or in consequ- ence of the construction of any of the werks by act authorised,or the execution of any of the powers by this act conferred norm the company, or the non-fulfilment of any of the oblig,tions by this act imposed upon the company, it shall be proved to the satisfaction of the Local Government Board, or of any cempetent authority that the waters of the canal or river Weaver have be- come so polluted as to be a nuisance injurious to health, the company shall forthwith execute all works, and do all things necessary for abating such nuisance, and for preventing such nuisance from again arising. (4). If by reason or in consequence of the construction of any of the works by this act authorised or the execution of any of the powjrs by this act conferred upon the company or the nonfulfilment of any of the obligations by this Act imposed upon the company, the District Council shall by any competent authority be ordered to clarify or otherwise deal with their sewage, then and in such case the company shall if so required by the Dis- trict Council construct and maintain such syphons or other ',rinks as may be necessary fot the purpose of en- abling such sewage t 3 be discharged into the estuary of the river Mersey, or otherwise dealt with. (5) Sub- sections 2, 3 and 4, of this section shall be in addition to and not in derogation of the rights, powers and privi- leges, and remedies of the District Council under or by virtue of the said agreement of the fifth day of May,one thousand eight hundred and ninety-one. (6) All works and things to be executed and done by the company under or by virtue of the provisions of this section shall be executed and ;lone at the expense in all things of the company. (7) Any difference from time to time arising between the District Council and the company, with reference to any of the matters referred to in thie sec- tion, sl:all in default of agreement be referred to a sole arbitrator to be appointed he the Local Government Board n n the apnlication of either 'party, and the cost of such reference shall be borne as he shall direct." A further clause has been inserted, with reference to the byelaws from the'Bridgewator Canals, and also one that the company shall preserve and maintain a roadway over their property, from Weston Peint to Runcorn, to be four feet wide. In addition the Clerk said that at the end of the clause enabling the Company to make bye- laws in relation to the Bridgewater Canals, the follow- ing words had been inserted :—" Nothing in this Act or in any byelaw made in pursuance thereof shall take away, alter, or prejudicially affect any casement belong- ing to or claimed by the Rural District Council of B un- corn, and an alteration had also been made whereby the company were to reserve and maintain a footway not less than 4ft. over the property of the Company from Weston Point to Runcorn. The clause generally spoken of as the Retardation Clause had been struck out which left the Company in the same position with regard to the overflow tidal or ether waters of the river Weaver, as they were before the Bill was introduced and was, they were to permit or cause all the tidal and other waters of the river Weaver which flowed into the canal to pass into the estuary of the Mersey at some point ther.in not being below the present outfall of the river Weaver. into the said estuary and at the same period of I each tide as such water would naturally pass into the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting,: of this Board was held on Tues- day eyening at the Town Hall. Tnere were present Mr. Pritchard (chairman). the Rev. Dr. Gastaldt, Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P., and Mr. G. Moss (clerk).</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE _ COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the meeting of this committee showed that 85 parents bad been summoned, 56 attended and magisterial proceedings were ordered to be taken against 7. The visitors' reports showed that during June the number of children on the rolls,was 3393 as against 3401 with the corresponding period of last year. and the aver- age attendance was 2869 showing a percentage of 84.5 which was considered satisfactory.—The Rev. Dr. Gas- taldi in moving the confirmation of the minutes said there was one fact that ought to receive attention namely the non-attendance of children of boat-people. 'They had children 12 or 13 years old who could not read or write because there waa no proper provision made for their education. If something could not be done they would b‘ brought up in darkness and ignorance, and if some step could be taken which would force the children into school it would be a good work accomplished.— The Chairman said fresh legislation was required to prevent the children going on board. Until that was done they cou:d not have proper control over them.—Dr. Gastaldi : It is a great pity.—The motion was seconded by Mr. Norman and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>SCHOOL CLEANING.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL CLEANING.</p>
        <p>The tender of Mr. D. Lea for lime washing the Vic- toria-road, Granville-street, and Mill Brow, schools was accepted. There were four tenders under considera- tion.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION,</p>
        <p>The resignation of Miss Frances M. Sproston, assistant mistress of Granville-street school, was accepted.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL EXTENSIONS FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved that the ground plan of new buildings in extension of Victoria-road School. be anc, is hereby approved and that the Clerk is authorized to in- struct Messrs. F. and G. Holme, the Board's architects, to prepare the further plans and extensions necessary,to be sent to the Education Department. The subject was before the Board at the close of last year, and plans had been prepared for the erection of a school on the vacant land in Victoria-road. Those were not generally ap- proved of, and they were asked to reconsider the matter. This year it was suggested that they should join the two schools or extend the present one. The new building consisted et four class-rooms on the ground floor, and four class-rooms above, providing accommodation for 176 boys and 176 girls. It might not be thoroughly understood by the ratepayers h )w the necessity for the provision of the schools was caused, and be might ex plain that the Brunswick schools could not be taken over by the Board as they were required for connectional pur- poses, and Granville-st. could not be continued in its present form. The rent they were paying for the use of Brunswick schools would go a long way towards paying the principal and interest of the new schools. The mat- ter had cost the Committee a considerable amount of time and thought. The Educational Department had repeatedly applied to the Board for them to forward plans and specifications of the new buildings. They had to consider the state of the town, and they had come to the decision that this was the best arrangement to be arrived at. He would have been glad if there had been a larger number of the members of the Board present on the occasion of the passing of such an important matter. He proposed that the resolution be adooted. Mr. Norman, in seconding, said they need not have any misgiving as to the policy from an educational stand- point. The extension would bring the Board school up to modern requirements, and would meet their require- ments for some time to come.—Dr. Gastaldi thought that the building was a necessity for the education of the children? of Runcorn, and everything they had done was a step in the right direction.—The resolution was then carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DEFEATED EDUCATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE DEFEATED EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY SIR J. T. BRUNNER, M.P.</p>
        <p>Sir J. T. Brunner, M.P., took part en Saturday in laying the foundation stones of new schoolrooms in con- nection with the Unitarian Chapel, Nantwich. In the course of the ceremony, Sir John, in acknowledging a vote of thanks, said he was present that day with his heart full of warm feelings of affection towards the Uni- tarians of Nantwich and their cause It was a fact that those who had been brought up in the Unitarian faith took more than the share due to their numbers of the public work of the country. Unitarians were often held up to scorn because:it was said they were so respectable. (Laughter.) There were so few poor amongst them, and many who were rich. He commended to the con- sideration of young men the fact that a boy being brought up amongst Unitarians put into him that cour- age and that independence of thought which, if only backed by perseverance and good health, would almost inevitably lead to fortune, and if not to fortune, at any rate would lead to a determination,and usually to a suc- cessful determination, to share in the public work of his country. In the next generation he felt sure that in the sense of great fortune and in the sense of doing a public duty, Unitarianism wmuld be ten times as respect- able as it was to-day. (Laughter and applause.) , Speaking at a public meeting in the Town Hall in the evening, Sir J. 'l'. Brunner, referring to the question of education, said that the country was no longer con- fronted by the insidious and mischievous proposals of the Education Bill, but it behoved them, between now and next year,to consider what should be their position in regard to the great question of education. The withdrawal of the Education Bill had been a great triumph for the cause of education. (Cheers.) A month ago none of them dared to hope that tho Govern- ment, with a majority of 152 to begin with,would have fallen so disastrously. Whether that defeat was due to Liberalism or to the jealousies which the bill anused amongst various local authorities he did not venture , to say, but there was no doubt that the attack made upon the board school system justified him in saying that the bill was a bad and mischievous bill, and that its withdrawal was a triumph for education. (Cheers.) If the proposal to limit the national expenditure to 29;. per child in average attendance had been carried, the Government would have put them in the shameful posi- tion of being the only country in the world fixing a limit to national expenditure upon education. It had always been said that the Government of this country was in the hands of the richest men in the country, but the fact did not appear to be ?ti widely known that nearly three-fourths of the men in. the House of Com- mons, and nine-tenths of the men in the Cabinet, had had assistance to free education in amounts multiplying over and over again the amounts offered to the poor,and the proposed grants to the poor were the smaller and miledembayrytehlelewd etahlatthitehsetspeemopelne in meaner the bec country.aus e they w (Cheers.)ere should have descended so low as to refuse a sum beyond this poor pittance to the children of those who had hard work to provide not merely education but actually food and clothing for their children. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>Messrs. Budd, Johnsons, and Jecks, solicitors,London, state that the amount upon which estate duty has been paid under the will of Colonel North is £153,091, and not £263,866 as has been reported. The latter figure represents only personal estatn in the United Kingdom. Colonel North had a considerable amount of property abroad,and even the £453,091 does not include his real estate or immovable property abroad. THE PUBLIC ARE WARNED against the use of Foreign cocoas conta fling alkali, producing highly coloured prepara- tions offered as '• pure eocoa." Cadbury's Cocos is absolutely pure and unadulterated, and is therefore the best. The 1" Lancet" describes it as " the standard of highest purity at ' present attainable,"—Advt.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday,present Mr.J.W.Knowles (chairman), Rev. J. S. Simpson, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., and S. Yarwood, the members of ths Rural Council, Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), and Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>THE LADIES' COAtAIITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of a meeting of this Committee showed that they requested that they should be allowed occa- sionally to visit the male side of the house.—This was granted on the motion of Mr. Brandreth, seconded by Mr. Roylance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CHILDREN'S TREAT.</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S TREAT.</p>
        <p>The Master announced that on Tuesday week he in- tended to take the children of the house for their annual seaside trip. Southport would be visited, and if any Guardian wishedto attend arrangements would be made. —Mr. Heywood said he had obtained in subscriptions from the Guardians £4, donations from the magistrates £lO, which with a balance in hand made £l7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>FINANCIAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywood, chairman of the Financial Commit'ee, stated that they had a balance in the books of £4.050 up to date. The quarter's accounts passed for payment amounted to £1,307, amongst which were the salaries of the officials and the different goods used in the mainten- ance of the house. Tne report was adopted. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CHILDREN AND OUTSIDE SCHOOLING.</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN AND OUTSIDE SCHOOLING.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote on the -Ist inst. saying that with the present state of the law the Guar- dians of the Union had no authority to make a payment as suggested to the funds of the Dutton School Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles E. Linaker, clerk to Dutton School Board, under date July 6:11, wrote saving that the !etter with reference to the workhouse children was laid before the Board at a meeting on Saturday, when after some discussion it was decided to defer the consideration thereof until next meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker, writing as clerk of the Daresbury United District School Board, on the same subject, said that with reference to the four children which the Board wanted to send to the Preston Brook school it was resolved that they be accepted at the institution.—The Clerk pointed out that the matter stood in this way. The infants would be taken to the Preston Brook schools and the question of the girls would be dealt with at the next meeting.—No comments wera made by the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>DRINK AND INSANITY</head>
        <p>DRINK AND INSANITY</p>
        <p>A circular letter was read from Mr. A. J. Wood, clerk to the Guardians of the Richmond Union, asking the Board to support the following resolutions and to take such action as might be deemed expedient with a view to obtaining an amendment of the existing law, to meet such cases as were indicated in the resolution :—" That in the opinion of this Board provision should be made for the detention and cure of persons who manifest symptoms of insanity when they have taken small quan- tities of intoxicating drinks and at frequent intervals.— Mr. Taylor: They are short of work in that Union, I should think.—Mr. Weir: What does it mean ?—A member: It means that everybody who takes drink shall be considered insane.—Mr. Pilling : Let it lie on the table.—Mr. Heywood proposed that the letter be sent to the Ladies Committee. (Laughter.) The letter was laid on the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DETERMINED SUICIDE OF A RUNCORN MA,N.</head>
        <p>DETERMINED SUICIDE OF A RUNCORN MA,N.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning James Graham Hutton, com- mitted suicide at his residence in Greenway-road, in a very determined manner. He was left alone in a bed- room about eight o'clock on that morning, and shortly, afterwards his brother heard a report, and on going up- stairs, found him dead, with the contents of a revolver discharged in his left side. Dr. Ormsby was called in, but could not render assistance, death having taken place. Deceased had been ill for six weeks with ner- vous depression,and had also received a letter intimating that his place would be filled up at the local offices of the Manchester Ship Canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>INQUEST.</head>
        <p>INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Worsley held the inquest on deceased on Monday morning at the Police Station.—Frederick William Hut- ton, a clerk, residing at 95, Greenway-road, Runcorn, was the first witness called. He said that deceased was 31 years old, and lived with him and his mother. On Saturday morning deceased was sleeping in a room by himself. Witness heard his brother get up and go to their -nother's room about six o'clock. He heard him crying but there was no dispute. He had since learned from his mother that deceased was very despondent, and she tried to cheer him up. About 7.30 deceased became quieter and went back to his bedroom while his mother came downstairs. Deceased had been in a low mental way and had not been to his employment for six weeks. He broke down through overwork, and had been attended since by Dr. Robinson. Before breakfast he listened at his brother's door but hearing all was quiet, did not go in, thinking his brother was asleep. He and his mother sat down to breakfast, and as soon as they did so they heard a shot. Witness rushed upstairs and found his brother lying by the side of the bed on the floor with his head in a pool of blood. There was a re- volver by his side. He called to the deceased but there was no response. A neighbour I and Dr. Ormsby, were sent for but when the doctor came,life wax extinct. The revolver did not belong to the house and he was not aware his brother had it. Deceased had never threatened to destroy him- self but had often wished that he was dead. There was a letter which had upset him, which was from his employers and was received on WednesJay last. Wit- ness then handed the coroner a letter which was read. The letter was dated July Ist and was written from the Manchester Ship Canal Company (Rridgewater Canals Undertaking), Runcorn. The following is a copy : " Dear sir,—l am in receipt of your letter of yesterday's date and regret to hear that you have had a relapse. I did not consider when you saw me on Friday evening that you were strong enough to resume duty and there- fore did not feel any surprise when you failed to come to the office on Monday morning. I regret to say that I shall not be able to keep your situation open longer, but I will ask the manager if he will allow you your salary for the current month in addition to the two months you have already received. Yours truly, J. Meadow- croft.'</p>
        <p>The Coroner: I suppose the situation could not have I been kept open indefinitely.—Witness : That is not a 1 matter lamin a position to speak of. The letter made 1 deceased very despondent.—The Coroner: I suppose, . previous to the receipt of the letter he had been in a , very despondent state.—Witness : Yes, he had been, but still he was trying to get better and we had arranged ' to take a sea trip together.—The Coroner : Did he say 1 anything to you about the lezter.—Witness : When my 1 brother read it his face was a perfect blank. He got up , and went to the door and leaned on it and said. " This is a finisher." He seemed quite overcome. We have heard him several times talking to himself and we could hear him say "Oh that dreadful letter ; that dreadful ' letter."—The Coroner : How long had he been employed by the Company—Witness : About 15 years.—The Coroner : That will mean practically all his working life.—Witness : Yes.—The Coroner : What did Dr. Robinson say about him.—Witness : Dr. Robinson said that he was not fit to resume work, and told him so on Wednesday night. He left his work six weeks ago because he was physically incan- able. Since then he had tried twice to resume but had to give up. The Coroner: He was broken down physic- ally and mentally and the doctor would not hear of him going back?—Witness: The long hours and close confine- ment broke down his physical health.—The Coroner: What were his hours.—Witness : Nominally from 9 o'clock until six, practically from 8.30 or 9 o'clock in the morning until 9.30 at night. Of course, that was not every day but frequently.—The Coroner: What about Saturday.—Witness : He got a Saturday afternoon off about once a month. The Coroner: Not more.—Wit- ness : No, sir. Ido not think so, taking the 12 months through.—The Coroner: Have you anything else to add. —Witness: I can only say this, that until he got that letter he bad some hope—it t was only small—after that ,he had none, absolutely none. I might mention that Dr. Robinson gave my brother a certificate to hand to his employers stating the condition he was in, and that it was absolutely necessary for his recovery that he silould have a more varied occupation.—A Juror : Did he. get any extra pay for the extra work.—Witness : Not a penny.—A Juror: What was his salary.—Witness : £75 per year. Dr. Ormsby stated that he was called in on the Satur- day morning and found the deceased lying by the side of the bed with his head in a pool of bl)od. Deceased had been dead about half-an-hour. The cause of death was due to a revolver shot which had penetrated the heart and lungs.—P.C. Gregory stated that he was also called in after the doctor. He found the revolver (produced) which was loaded and had had one chamber discharged and a box containing 44 cartridges.—Dr. Robinson said he had attended deceased who had complained that the work had been too much for him. He complained of sensa- tions in the head, but he was quite sane and had no de- lusions. He was a man of great intellect and possessed a cultivated mind. The Coroner : What was his state. —Doctor Robinson : The effects of overwork and close confinement. Continuing Dr. Robinson stated that he wrote deceased a certificate suggesting that a change and a more varied employment might do him good, and he warned the family to look after him. He salw him on Wednesday, and talked to deceased about his prospects, Deceased had received a letter which had greatly de- pressed him, ond he (witness) tried to cheer him up. The Coroner after reviewing the evidence, said what the jury had to do was to decide the cause of death. That would be easy, for there was no doubt that he had committed suicide. They..had to consider also what state of mind he was in. It they thought ho was a sane man and voluntarily destroyed his own life, they would . return a verdict of " felo-de-se." If on the other hand, they thought that his trouble, particularly the letter he had received, weighed upon him .they would return as their verdict that he committed suicide while in an un- L sound mind. The jury returned a verdict of suicide while in an unsound mind, and the Coroner was in- formed by a majority of the jery that they considered , the letter deceased ha l received a cruel letter seeing that he had been in the service 15 years.</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.-OLD-AGE PENSION COMMISSION.</p>
        <p>Answering Mr. Dalziel in the House of Commons on Monday, Mr. BRODRICK said no British regiment had received orders to take part in the advance on Dongola. Mr. BALFOUR, also replying to a question put by Mr. Dalziel, said he could not state approximately the monthly cost of the expedition to Dongola, inasmuch as the Egyptian Government were responsible for the con- duct of the campaign, and the details of the expenditure had not hitherto been asked for. Lord Cromer had been commenicated with on the subject. 9 Lord G. FILMILTON moved a resolution to the effect that, Her Majesty having directed a military expedition of her native forces charged upon the revenues of India to be despatched to Africa in aid of the Egyptian troops, the H use consented that the ordinary pay of any troops so despatched, as well as the ordinary charges of any vessels belonging to the Government of India that might be employed in this expedition, which would have been charged upon the resources of India if such troops or vessels had remained in that country or seas adjacent, should continue to be so chargeable, provided that if it should become necessary to replace the troops or vessels so withdrawn the expebse of replacing them should be repaid outs of moneys provided by Parlia- ment. Mr. MORLEY moved as an amendment— "That it is inexpedient that any portion or the charges of the Indian force that iq being despatched to Africa in aid of Egyptian troops, whether ordinary charges or extraordinary, should be imposed upon the revenues of India." Mr. J. M. Maclean seconded the arn,mdment, which, in the course of the debate, was supported by several Conservative MP M hers. On a division the amend- ment was rejected by 275 votes to 190, a majority of 85.</p>
        <p>Mr. HULSE asked the First Lnrcl of the Treasury when</p>
        <p>he would be in a position to inform the Flous; nf the names of those appointed to the Couimigsion promised by the Government to inquire into the subject of old-age pensions. Mr. A. BALFOUR : Lord Rothschild has expressed his readiness to preside over the Commission. The other members are Sir F. Mowatt.of the Treasury ; Sir (I,iirte- nay Boyle, of the Board of Trade ; Mr. Speacer Walpole, of the Post Office; tyfr.Finlaison,actuary for the National Debt ; Mr. Brabrook,Registrar of Friendly Societies ;Mr. George King, actuary of the Atlas Insurance CorAany ; Mr. A. W. Watson, actuary of the Manchester Unity ; and Mr. Alfred Chapman, parliarnentaly agent for the Foresters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Replying to Mr. Stephens in the House of Commons on Tuesday,Mr. RITCHIE said the relatives or representa- tives of passengers lost in the wreck of the Drummond Castle would be allowed to take part in the inquiry and to produce evidence connected with the circumstance; of the command and other causes leading to the dis- aster. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. CURZON, replying to Mr. Fl. Roberts, said the withdrawal of the Turkish troops from Crete was not one of the four points demanded by the Powers. In censequence, however, of the representations made at Constantinople, orders had been sent to the military authorities in Crete to suspend all military operations unless attacked. The House afterwards went into Committee on the Finance Bill. Mr.LLOYD-GEORGE had previously moved an amendment excluding from duty tea grown in any part of the British Empire, and Mr. H. LEWIS, on behalf of Mr. Lloyd-George, now asked leave to withdraw it. Mr. J. Lowreen objected, and after a discussion the ameedment was negatived without a division. Mr. H. LEWIS moved an amendment to the effect that the duties on the higher-priced teas should be raised and the duties upon lower-priced teas lewered. This also was withdrawn. Mr. LLOYD-GEORGE moved to reduce the tea duty from fourpence to twopence per pound,but-the proposal was defeated by 263 votes to 96,and the clause was agreed to by 228 votes to 37. Mr. LLOYD GEORGE also moved an amendment providing that where pro- perty to which the husband was not entitled in his own right passed on his death to his wife she should pay the estate duty on the whole property, I?tit this was rejected by 146 votes to 59, and the clause was carried by 175 votes to 75. In the HOLM of Lords the Working Men's Dwellings Bill passed through Cemmittee without amendment, and was then referred to the Standing Committee. On the nentinn for the third reading of the Diseases of Animals Bill, Lord Herschel' moved an amendment to the first clause with the object of providing that if it should be represented to Her Majesty by an Address of either House of Pa.rliaittint that any particular colony or country was free from cattle disease, and that animals might be admitted from such colony or country without any danger of introducing disease or spreading disease, it should be lawful for Her Majesty in Council by Order to allow animals, or any specified kind of animals, to be landed without being subject to the provisions of the Act, provided that the admission of such animals should he subject to such regulations as to the route by which the animals were conveyed to this country, quarantine, or otherwise, as the Board of Agriculture might direct. After a debate, in which the Earl of Rosebery and Lord Salisbury took part, the amendment was rejected by 103 votes to 26, and the hill then passed. WEDNESDAY. The House of Commons on Wednesday resumed in committee the consideration of the financial proposals of the Government as embodied in the Finance Bill. On clause 16, exempting from estate duty under certain cnaditiona articles of national, scientific, and historic in- terest, Sir W. HARCOURT said that in the Act of 1894 exemption only was given to articles dedicated to the public, but in this clause the exemption was given to articles which still remained in private hands. Sir M. H. Beace replied that the exemption trom duty would only be till they were sold or came into possession of a person competent to dispose of them. Sir W. HARCOURT said the policy of the Act of 189 t was.as nearly as could be, to make all property pay alike, and if they now &amp;e- -lected a particular class of property, and that belonging mainly to rich people, and made special exemptions applicable.thev would create a sense of inequality which he thought would be very unfortunate. Sir M. H. Beam said he estimated, when he introduced the Budget. that the proposed exemptions would lead to a loss of £200,000 but he was now of opinion that it would not amount to half that sum. After further discussion the clause was carried by 178 votes to 74. On clause 19. which fixed the income ten at eightpence in the pound. Mr. H. Lewis moved an amendment with the object of establishing a graduated income tax. He propored that on incomes under £5OO per annum the tax should be at the rate of fourpenne. on incomes between £5OO and £750 at the rate of sixperce, and on incomes between £750 and £l,OOO at the rate of eightpence. After discussion the amendment was rejected by 168 votes to 84. Mr. WHIT- TAKER proposed that the income tax should be reduced from Bi. to but the amendment was defeated by 221 votes to 58.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>T HURSD SY.</head>
        <p>T HURSD SY.</p>
        <p>Replying to Mr. Warr in the House of Commons on Thursday, the ATTORNEY GENERAL said chances were under consideration affecting the present assize system, and as to Lancashire, it was hoped that they might go far to meet the suggestion which had recently been made with regard to the A4RiZP9 in that county. Mr. STEPHENS asked the First Lord of the Treasury whether he was aware that the inhabitants of the island of MoT(*sie were liable to water famines, and suggested that it would be a graceful thing to provide them with the mean! of obtaining a better water supply in return f3r their kindness to the friends and relatives of the neonle lost in the wreck of the Drummond Castle. Mr. A. J. BALFOUR said the Government had no knowledge of the matter referred to in the first'part of the question. The form of acknowledgment of the kindness and hum- anity displayed by the people and the authorities of Moli;ne on the occasion of the recent disaster was being ronsidered, but he could do no more at present than thank the hon, member for his suggestion.</p>
        <p>In the house of Lords, Lord Harris moved the second reading of the Aaricultural Land Rating Bill. Lord Barrer moved the rejection of the measure. Lord Salis- bury defended the action of the Government. The bill was strongly condemned by Lord Rosebery. On a division the motion for the second reading was carried by 153 votes to 32. On the report of amendments to the Marriage with a Deceased Wife's Sister Bill. Viscount Halifax moved an amendment the effect of which was to declare invalid marriages with a deceased wife's sister which had been contracted prior to the passing of the Act. The amendment was rejected by 90 votes to 47. Viscount Galway proposed the omission of that part of the second clarise which exempted from liability clergymen of the Churnh of England for with. holding the rights and privileges of Church membership from persons Bring together in marriages made valid by this Act. On a division the membership was carried by a majority of nino. The Bishop of Salisbury moved the insertion of a clause providing that no man who had been divorced for or committed adultery with his wife's sister should he permitted to contract a marriage with herond any such pretended marriage should be void. The amendment was negatived without a division. A new clause providing that nothing in the Act should affect, the law of marriage in any part of the Queen's dominions outside the United Kingdom was agreed to, and the report was then received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SHOCKING DIS6OVERY NEAR</head>
