      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Although winners of medals and diplomas at the Chicago Exhibition of 1893 have had to wait three years before receiving the prizes awarded to them, even this period has not been sufficient to prevent the designer of the medal from making an egregious blunder in the in-. scriptien. On tbe reverse side of the medal it is recorded that Christopher Colornbus landed in aincecxcii, instead of mcccxcn.—i.e, 1892 instead of 1492.</p>
        <p>Fifteen years ago Mr. J. Condor, residing at Hairnet House, Chelmsford, lost a valuable diamond ring in his stables, and in spite of a most diligent search no trace of the ring could be found. A few days ago the gar- dener, in turning over one of the flow.er-beds, discovered the long-lost ring. It is believed that It was carried with the manure from the stables to the flower-bed.</p>
        <p>Princess Maud, accomP.anied by her father and mother, the Prince and Prmc.ss of Wales, has just paid a visit of inspection .to her future home, Appleton Hall. The place has been in the hands of the decorators and builders for some time, getting ready for the approach- ing honeymoon of the Royal couple. The Royal party (aecordir,g to the "Daily Chronicle ") expressed them- selves well pleased with the character and progress of the extensive alterations and improvements that are being made in the building and its surrounding grounds.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>EVERY PERSON'S FUTURE.</head>
        <p>EVERY PERSON'S FUTURE.</p>
        <p>Every person's future is, to a certain extent, in hi own hand. Many a future which might; have been bril- liant has been darkened by lack of the spirit of self-help Many a home might have been gladdened by life and health and comfort, has been saddened by poverty, illness and death, in many cases wholly preventible. The spring and summer months are, from a sanitary point of view, among the most important of the year. Due care may now mean a healthy and happy summer and autumn, and robust constitution to meet the trials of next winter. Neglect may now mean a joyless sum- mer, a trying autumn, and a broken constitution to succumb under the attacks of any exceptional trial. There are indeed few that do not feel the system needs recruiting. With some it is "only a little cough," "tendency to bronchitis," and others, " not quite up to the mark," " a sort of all over sinking feeling," " tog weak to do anything about the house," "these recurrind headaches,"" no appetite for his food," atc. These an a dozen others are eommon expressions which all point to the urgent need of same good tonic. What change of air can do for you in a few months' time—if you are no worse then than you are now—a course of some good tonic mixture, a reliable medicine of established reputa- tion and of proved virtue will do now. There are several tonic mixtures to he had, but none which have been SO uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic.</p>
        <p>It is the Best REMEDY of the age for</p>
        <p>WEAKNESS, NERVOUSNESS,</p>
        <p>LOW SPIRITS,</p>
        <p>MELANCHOLY,</p>
        <p>CHEST AFFECTION, LOSS OF APPETITE, INDIGESTION, AND BLOOD DISORDERS. At this season of the year no one should be without GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. A course taken now will be valuable in giving tone to the system, new life to the blood, and in bracing the nerves. Praised by everybody. Avoid Imitations. See the r.nic Gwilym Evans" on the Label, Stamp and Bottle. Prices :—Bottles, 2s. 9d. ; double size. 45. 6d. To be had of all Chemists, or direct from the Proprietors : QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING Co., LTD., LLA.NELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED BEGGING-LETTER IMPOSTORS AT WARRINGTON. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday morning, Charles Frederick Harrison and John Gorton were charged with begging in Bold-st. on Sunday after- noon.—They pleaded guilty.—P.C. Clough said that on Sunday he observed the defendants loitering suspici- ously about Wilson Patten-st. and watched them. They turned into Bold-st. and he followed. He saw them stop at the door of the Rev. W. H. Drummond's house, and hand a letter to that gentleman, asking for assistance. • Witness came up to them and Mr. Drummond told him he knew nothing about them. The letter in question was handed to the Bench and read by the magistrates, who commented upon its literal accuracy and good penmanship.—lt read as follows :—" July 5th,1896. Reverend and Dear Sir.— Myself aed friend. both Unitarians and educated men, having journeyed (on foot)from Liverpool since morning on our way back to Manchester, being quite exhausted with our journey and previous search for two friends in Liverpool, whom we failed to trace, make this appeal to your humanity and generosity for the hare means of a night's lodgings and a little food, after which we shall be able to finish our journey without troubling anyone.; We started off this morning without one penny—break- fastless—our last shilling having been spent last night in the barest necessities. In a predicament so pressing and exceptional we are driven to make this appeal te you as the most consistent and honourable course, the many intellectual conflicts on doctrinal points with Trinitar- ians which the writer has had from time to time shutt- ing him out in particular from expecting any kind of favour from members of so-called orthodox ' denomi- nations. Until recently I spent all my life in Sheffield, was for many years chief order clerk for Messrs. John Crowley, and have attended regularly for a long time past the Upper Chapel, Norfolk-street, and occasionally at Upper Thorpe Chapel, and know well the Revs. Eli Fay, J.P. Bland, Manning, Charles Peach, Blazebv, Page-Hopps, and others. Reverend Sir, this procedure will seem to you most singular and unwarrantable, and the only excuse is the hard necessity of the case, and any assistance, however little, you may generously accord to us will in no wise be abused, but gratefully re- ceived.—Yours most respectfully, Charles Frederick Harrison and John Gorton.—P.S. Pray pardon such an Intrusion on your privacy, especially on a Sunday."— The men asserted that the letter was true and written by Harrison.—Both were sent to gaol for seven days. ANOTHER CASE OF INDECENCY IN THE PARK. On Thursday morning at the Warrington Borough ?slice Court before Mr. S. Chaedley (in the chair), and Alderman Sutton, a middle-aged man, named Thomas Drinkwater, was charged with indecently exposing him- self in the Park on Wednesday with intent to insult cer- tain females.—Defendant said he would plead guilty- as he knew nothing about it.—The Town Clerk prose- cuted and said offences of this kind were getting too fre- quent in the Park. Parents, instead of finding it a pleasant place to send their children began to look upon the Park with some degree of fear. If the Bench were satisfied that the prisoner had committed the offence he was charged with, he (the Town Clerk) asked that he might be punished with the utmost rigour of the law, which was three months' hard labour. Prisoner was complained of on Wednesday morning and a watch was kept. He was found conducting himself indecently in the presence of children.—Mr. Ashton, park keeper, cor- roborated, and said that he saw prisoner, himself, com- mit the offence. There were children of from 3 years to T years of age close by.—lnspector Spinks informed the magistrates that prisoner was not a Warrington man but had come from Southampton three days ago. He had been tramping about the country for the last seven years. —Prisoner, who pleaded that he bad never troubled the police before, was given the maximum penalty, three months bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr. J. Burgess (etrtirman), Mr. S.</p>
        <p>Chandlev and Mr. T. Bin;hall.</p>
        <p>A WALKING DAY PICKPOCKET.—John McLaughlin, a married man, was charged with frecy tenting Market Gate between 9.45 and 10.15 on Friday morning with intent to commit felony.—Detective Ballow said that at 945 on Friday he was on duty in plain clothes at Market Gate in company with Detective Roper. They observed prisoner stating suspiciously. He attempted to pick the pockets of three ladies. They got close to him and caught him In the aet.—Detective Roper corroborated and prisoner was com- mitted to gaol for one month's hard labour. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—WiIIiam Ross, for being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street. at 3 o'ciock on Friday afternoon was fined 10s. and costs; for committing the same offence in the same street on the evening of Friday, Thomas Walsh was fined ss. and costs.—Serge snt Ch Intler proved both cases; Elizabeth Elll•on was charged with committing a like offence in Cambria-place at 9-20 on Friday night. She was further charged with using threats to her husband. She was suffering greatly from the effe,ts of drink and be- haved very violently in the dock.—P.C. Barnett proved the first offence and defendant's husband t tted that he was separated from defendant but she came and annoyed him and on the night In question threatened to hay his life. He re- quested the constable to remove her. He was afraid she ' would do him harm.—She was fined be. and costs or seven days, and Mr. H. Taylor (Police Court Missionary) promised to look after her and try to get her into a home.</p>
        <p>113NDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman). Nir F. Menke, Mr.</p>
        <p>T. Birchall, -and -Aldermait-Wright.</p>
        <p>, LICF-43E TRA.NSFERS.—John Taylor, was granted the trans- fer the license of the Unicorn Inn. from lienry Rowley ; Mr. Spencer Wallhead. solicitor, applied for the transfer of ttto I.censes held by the late Mr. Peter Leigh to his widow, Mrs. Sarah Jane Leigh. This was also granted. A DII3ERTER.,—John Wash was charged with being a de- serter trona the Ist Battalion of the Connatuht Rangers, stationed at Strensall, since June 13th—A. Sergeant from the barracks proved defendant's identity and he was handed over to the milit lry authorities.</p>
        <p>Tun 9 D A.Y.—Before Councillor.Henr y Roberts (chairman),</p>
        <p>and Dr. Starkey Smith</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. —William Lythgoe was charged with being drunk and disonlerly in S. Jones-street, the previous night. The. prisoner who now made his 19th appearance. was threatening to fight a number of nersons. P.O. Metcalfe proved the case, a.n: t,-!e prisoner was fined ss. and •tosts or in default seven days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>W ,:DNESDXY.—Before Messrs. John Fairclou.qh (in the chair), J. Geddes, J. H. Fleming. and S_NVright,</p>
        <p>THEFT FTVONK A RESTAURANT.—Mary Ann Ramat, a dis- solute-looking woman, was charged with stealing on the 3rd inst.. from Dean's Dining Rooms, in Peter-street, a brass weight, value 2s.—Detective Sergeant Monaghan said that about 6 o'clock on Friday, the 3rd July, defendant went Into th 3 Dining Rooms. After she lett. the brass weight was missed and information given to the police, who eventually arrested the woman. Defendant stated that she had had d -ink cr would not have done it and that the devil must have got hold of her. She had thrown the weight away beino, unable to sell it.—The magistrates sent her to gaol for 14 e.ays' hard labour. ALLEGED THEFT OF OLD !HON.—Two yonths from Turner- st , Philip Thompson and Richard Weaver were charged with haying in their possession a quantity of iron, the same supposed to have been stolen from the turks lat-ly occupied by John- Grant. in Lythgoes-larie. Detective-Sergeant Monaghan said that from what came to his knowledge on Tuesday afternoon he arrested the tvlo prisoners in Turner- st.on Tuesday evening. They were rubbingstone hawkers, an he asked them to account for the iron they had during thd afternoon. Weaver took witness to Howffey fields and sheowed him where the iron was buried. They had concealed it after failing to sell it at M'Clew's, in Queen-st. The iron which was in the form of " swift-holders." used in a wire works, was missing from Grant's place.—Prisoners were re- znanGed till to-day (Saturday) for further inquiries.</p>
        <p>CYCLE-0 RA. The finest embrocation in the world. Promptly restores tone to the tired or strained musc'e,=. qade byth Romocea Company, Limited, and put up in coil tnsible tubes, price 61. and 19.6 d. each. Sold by :—Mr. J ft. YouNG, Pharmaceutical hemist, Sankey-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>POWER THAT PULLS DOWN.</head>
        <p>THE POWER THAT PULLS DOWN.</p>
        <p>I want to present a single idea in the fewest and clear- est words at my command. Here goes for a try at it. From the time you are first able to stand on your feet, up to the time you can stand no longer, there is always la power pulling you backward and downward. You resist and it persists. It wins partial victories over you every day, and finally it lays you by the heels. Now, what is the name of that power? Don't be too quick with your answer. It is the over-confident cricketer • who gets bowled out. Perhaps the reading of these letters may help you. "In the spring of 1891," says a woman," my health, previously good begin to fail. lam naturally as ener- getic as most persons and enjoy being up and doing, but new for some reason I felt low, weak and tired. I had nd relish for food of any kind, and what I ate gave me pain at the chest and a feeling of tightness and oppres- sion around the waist, with shortness of breath.</p>
        <p>" After a time the pains went to my shoulders and all over me. The .listress after eating was so great that I hesitated before swallowing a mouthful, well knowing what the result would be. I took many medicines, but none of them gave me any ease. As time went on I be. same weaker and weaker, often leaving my household work for a bit so as to lie down on the couch and rest. And as this debility increased upon me my spells of work got shorter, and my spells of rest longer. "Sometimes feeling a trifle better and then again worse, this was practically my condition month after month. I saw a doctor, but his medicine did me little or no good. He said I was suffering from weakness,and would have to get my strength back gradually. The autumn and winter of 1891-2 slowly passed and I was about the same, only more thin and feeble. I had al- most given up all hope of getting really well again. "In April (1892) I was in our shop one day and heard a customer speak about mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and what remarkable cures of different ailments it had done in the district. • It may be the right thing for me,' said, and sent for it that very day. After taking one bottle I could eat better, without any distress or pain to come after it. With the additional food I gained strength and one week after another, while keeping on with Mother Seigel's Syrup, I found myself able to do more work and needing less rest between times. I took only the Seigel's ; no other medicine. I could stand and walk once more without thinking about it, and was soon in as good health and spirits as ever. Your are free to print my letter if you desire. (Signed) Mrs. Emma Cot- tingham, wife of G. W. Cottingham, Grocer and Iron- monger, Scotter, Lincoln, April 29th, 1895." " For over four years," says another, " I suffered from zonstant weakness. My natural strength was gone, and nothing I did or took seemed to bring it back. My food—and I ate but little—did not go to the spat, as we say. I was none the better or stronger for eating it. Indeed I was the worse; for it caused me great pain and distress in the stomach, chest, sides, and back. I was working in the mill and never quite gave up my employment ; but I did my work in the lace of pain and weakness. Finally, I was cured by Mother Seigel's Syrup. I heard of it by means of a little book. The Syrup stopped the pain ofter eating, and soon I was another and a brighter woman. My strength caine back, and I can walk, stand, and work with ease. (Signed) Mrs. Elizabeth Jackson, Silk street, Glossop, October 10th, 1895."</p>
        <p>Now what was the power that pulled these women down? " Weakness," you say, and they say "weak- ness." But what is weakness ? Is it a disease ? No, old age always brings it, and it is always one of the re- sults of disease. Strength, the opposite of weakness, is created only by diges'..ed fo-d. Nothing else under the sun will produce it. Lose the ability to digest your food and soon your legs tremble beneath you, your fingers lose their grip,your head its steadiness,and your mind its clearness and courage. The word " weakness" comes from a Saxon Word meaning to yield, to fail, to give way. By its wondrous virtue in correcting tha machinery of digestion and enabling the system to get "the good" of its daily food—by this, I say, Mother Seigel's Syrup, used in time, overcomes weakness and restores strength.</p>
        <p>But, mark you ! there is a mystery in this simple ex- planation, so deep we must reserve the discussion of it for another occasion.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE fiEkura OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During last week, which was the 26th, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warrington 37 13 Latchford 3 0</p>
        <p>Total 4B 13 The excess of births over deaths was 27. The death rate from all causes was equal to 118 per I,ooo,against 14.9 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 111 the corresponding week of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zyrsiotie diseases was equal to Nil per :,000, against 1•4 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against Nil the corresponding week of 1895._ The ages of mortality were— __</p>
        <p>0- 1 1-5- 5-15 15:-25 25-65 65 up. 4 - 1 1 5 .2 13</p>
        <p>The deaths included-2 from phthisis, 2 from old 'a.ge, 3 from lung disease, 4 from brain disease, '1 from heart disease, 1 from debility. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Erysipelas, 2 cases, Scarlet fever 1. case.</p>
        <p>Total number of patients in hospital on June 27th, 8.</p>
        <p>METEOROLOGY. —The mean temperature was 59 Fah. and therefore 51 degrees colder than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 70F., was recorded on the 28th inst., and the minimum temperature 45F., on the 22nd inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured •67 inch. The general direction was N.W. the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1035 mites.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GOHNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
