        <p>Then resort to Warner's Safe Cure, the only reliable remedy, as experiAncecl by thousands in all countries. "Is your breathing laboured ; have you lost your fresh colour, appetite, fish and strength? Do you suffer from palpitation of the heart? Are you wakeful at night and drowsy during the day? D your ankles or limbs swell ? Is your eyesight failing? Is there an occasional dull, heavy pain over the loins, and deposits in the urine?"</p>
        <p>If so, the kidneys—these important organs—are corn- plaining. No time should be lost. Procure and take Warner's Safe Cure (2s. 9d. and 4s. ed. of all dealers), a remedy which never fails in these diseases, as proved by the testimony of thousands.</p>
        <p>by Father Folding with the cross bearers and acolytes. The Borough Brass Band was in attendance. The chil- dren attending Orford-lane and King-street schools followed and were succeeded by Father Nugent's band from Liverpool. The Children of Mary the Mount Carmel Confraternity, and the men's Confraternity of the Sacred Heart followed next. The scholars attending the school in Liverpool- road also joined this procession and were in charge of the Rev. Father Ryan. A decorated trap brought up the rear. The League of the Cross Brass Band led up those connected with S. Mary's Buttermarket-street, and they were followed by the Latchford Roman Catholic School. The League of the Cross Fife Band, and the St. Joseph's (Manchester) also accompanied this portion of the pro- cession. Sme really fine banners were displayed, and the procession ov tihe whole was the best of the day. Services afterwards held in S. Alban's and S. Mary's Roman Catholic Churches. WYCLIFFE (CONGREGATIONAL).—Number of scholars on books, 300 boys, 270 girls ; average attendance, 317; number of teachers, 21 males 16 females; minister, Rev. John Yonge ; superinter:dent, George Wallington. The day's programme: The scholars as- sembed at 9 o'clock and proceeded to Bank Quay Station, from whence they were conveyed to Durham Hill by the 9.50 train. Tbe Wycliffe brass band headed the procession and accompanied the scholars t ) Dunham Hill. Here a field was obtained and enjoyments of a varied character indulged in. BOLD-STREET (WESLEYAN).—Number of scholars OH books, 165 boys, 184 girls; average attendance, 236; number of teaehers, 17 male, 18 female; number of books in library, 418; ministers, Revs. 0. Watkins, G. D. Mason, and W. G. Triggs; superintendent, Mr. W. Silcock ; secretaries, Messrs. F. T. Wilson, W. A. Mellor, and George Perkins. The scholars spent the day at Mr. Winstanley's Dairy Farm, Lymm, journeying by special train from Arpley at 9.53, and returning from Lynam at 8 p.m. They were provided with dinner and tea, and numerous games and sports indulged in and pleasant excursions made round the beautiful village. The scholars were accompanied by many friends. SILVER-STREET (WESLEYANS).—Number of scholars on books, 420; number of teachers, 35 ; number of books in library, 482; ministers, Rev. Owen Watkins, Rev. G. D. Mason, Rev. W. J. Triggs ; superintendents, Councillor J. Shaw-Greeen and Mr. J. W. Har7ard,J.P. The day's programme : The scholars left for Helsby at 9.30, and spent the whnle day there. Dinner and tea were provided for the children, acid games and other ar- rangements for their comfort were in plenty. SANKEY BRIDGES (WESLEYAN).—Number of scholars on books. 190 b0y5,139 girls; number of teachers,3o males. 10 females ; ministers, Revs. Owen Watkins, G. D. Masan, W. G. Triggs and J. Gunnell; superintendents, Messrs. Brough and Blythe. In the morning there was a procession,a start being made at 9.30, the route being along Liverpool-road and Lovely-lane. At about 330 the children walked in procession to Mr. Bennett% of Bewsey Hall,whe had kindly lent a field for the enjoy- ment of the scholars and friends. Dinner and tea were provided. Alter tea a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. and Mrs. Bennett and family for the generous dis- position shown towards the above school in allowing the scholars the privilege of so suitable a field for a day's pleasure. BOSTOCK-STREET (WESLEYA.N).—Number of scholars on books, 100; superintendent, Mr. Blakemore. The chil- dren at this school met at school at 10 o'clock on Friday morning, when the scholars received their tickets. After- wards they walked in procession through the neighbour- ing streets,singing as they went along,until they reached the field in Levely-lane, which had been kindly lent by Mr. Brough. At 12.30 all were supplied with a good dinner of ham sandwiches, and milk to drink. After dinner they enjoyed themselves with various games, cricket for the boys, and swings, skipping ropes, ice. for the girls, The teachers did all they could to make the day an enjoyable one. At 530 tea was provided, con- sisting of cake, ham sand wiches, bread and butter. After tea scrambling for nuts and sweets caused great fun. Games were indulged in until about 9 o'clock, when everyene went home thoroughly satisfied with the day's enjoyment. PRESIITIVE METHODIST (LEGH-ST.)—Number of scholars on books, 175; number of teachers, 10 male, 6 female; minister, Rev. W. J. Kirkland; superintendent, Mr. Walter G. Ward. After an amalgamated procession of the whole connection including Legh-st., Latchford, and Sharp-st., lead by the Post Office band, the scholars proceeded to Folly Farm kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. and Mrs. Wright. PRIMITIVE METHODISTS (LATCHFORD) .—Number of scholars on books, 132 ; average attendance,s2 morning, 95 afternoon ; ministers,of the circuit ; superintendents, Mr. J. Masser and Mr. E. Mair ; secretary, Mr. J. Cross. The scholars joined in the general procession and after- wards adjourned to a field near Black Bear Bridge, kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. Asthey. Numer- ous games were indulged in and a cricket match was played between the scholars and teachers.</p>
        <p>gMMANUEL REFORMED CUURCIII OF ENGLAND.—Num- ber of scholars on baoks. 93 male; 92 female ; average attendance, morning' 70 ; afternoon, 159 ; number of teachers, 8 male; 8 female; number of books in lib- rary, 500 ; the clergy is the Rev. E. T. Reed ; superin- tendent of boys' school, Rev. E. T. Reed ; superintendent of girls' school. nil ; secretary. Mr. A. Landon : trea- surer, Mr. William Atherton ; librarian, Mr. H. Gandy. The scholars assembled at school at 8.45. and jeined in the general procession. At 12 o'clonit they proceeded to Hill Warren (kindly lent by Mr. Derbyshire), on lorries, where both dinner and tea were provided. GOLUORNE-STREET (BAPTIST). —Number of scholars en books. 102 collectively ; average attendance, 35 morn- ings, 68 afternoon ; number of teachers, male 8, female 5; superintendesit, Mr. S. Fernley ; secretary, Mr. H. Buskby. The scholars proceeded by lurries to a field kindly lent by Mr. A. Pimlott, Burley Heyes, Appleton. Games much as cricket, racing, rounders, rambles, etc., were engaged in. The journey home was commenced at dusk, all well pleased with the day's outing. CAIRO STREET (UNITARIAN).—Number of scholars on books, 153 boys, 140 girls ; number of teachers, 10 mete, 13 female; number of books in library, 1,372; minister, Rev. W. H. Drummond, BA.; superintendents, Messrs. J. S. Hill and J. Pemberton ; secretary, Mr. T. C. Hol- lingsworth. The scholars and friends of this school had a field day at Helsby, when all thoroughly enjoyed them- selves. Dinner and tea were provided, and various games indulged in until dusk. ST. ANN'S CHURCH —Number of scholars On books, 197 boys, 240 girls ; afternoon school, average attendance, 120 boys; 180 girls; number of teachers, 8 male ;15 female ; ministers, Rev. J. Davies and the Rev. P. J. Byrne; superintendents, the Clergy, Vicar, afternoon, and the Rev. P. J. Bryne. morning, Miss Davies (girls), Miss It. Bunch (infants). The scholars assembled at school in Dannett-st. at a quarter to nine. Headed by the band, they marched to the Town Hall to join in the main procession. On return there was a service in the Church. At 3 p.m. tea was served in the schools, and afterwards the children marched te the field (kindly lent by Locomotives' Football Club), off Fay-lane. Games, &amp;c., were indulged in and prizes and bons were distributed. Sr. Josses (PnESBYTERIAN).—The scholars of the above school formed a proceseion of their own and pro- ceeded to Bank Quay Station where they departed, shortly after 9 o'clock for Frodsham. They were headed by the handsome school banner and were accompanied by the Rev. J. Warnock, (minister), Mr. H. Longdin (secretary), Mr. C. Sudlow (treasurer) Mr. Fletcher and others. Mr. T. Longdin was absent owing to Corpora- tion business in London. At Frodsham the scholars greatly enjoyed themselves. Dinner and tea were par- taken of at Overton Hills, and, needless to say were both heartily appreciated. T. PETER'S CIIIIRCH.—Number of scholars On books. 255 boys, 386 girls; average attendance, 210 boys, 300 male, 14 female; minis- Rev. S. J. Fleming, tgei;al R• , e numbervß. r e roefi val, teachers,vicar 9 curate- superintendents, the clergy. The day's programme in; chided: service in the chureh at 11.30 a.m., alter the- procession. The preacher was the vicar (Rev. B. Percival). The scholars re-assembled at 1.45 at school, and marched to Padgate to a field kindly lent by Mr. Cox. Tea was served on the field. Various games and races were indulged in. The proceedings were enlivened by musical selections. ST. Peam's.—Number of scholars on books. 183 boys, 300 girls ; number of teachers, 8 male, 20 female ; ministers, Rev. H. V. Pigot and Rev. H.Brierley ; super- intendents, Messrs. A. J. Jolley and J. Lawless. The day's programme : The scholars attending the above school took part in the general procession and had a special service at St. Paul's. The afternoon and even- ing were spent in having tea at the schools, and sports, in the field adjoining St. James' Church. WARRINGTON RAGGED SUNDAY SCHOOL.—Number on books, 346 ; boys 149, girls 197 ; average, 187 ; teachers, males 6, females 12 ; superintendent, Mr. James Skel- land ; assistant. Mr. F. J. Owen ; treasurer, Mr. F. R. Roberts, M.A., L.L.B. ; financial secretary, Mr. Thomas Cowdell; school secretary, Mr. W. Sparks. The scholars assembled at the schoolroom at 8 a.m. to receive clothing given by generous friends. The fatherless girls were each provided with a new pair of shoes and a dress, out of the funds of the school, the sewing being done by some of the teachers and elder scholars. This added greatly to the appearance of the children. On leaving the school a scone or bun was given to each child, and they then proceeded to the park, headed by the school hand, and joined in the procession round the town. Afterwards the children marched to a field in Lovely lane, kindly lent by Mr. Bennett, where dinner, consist- ing of meat pies was served. The afternoon was spent in various games and amusements for the children, rac- ing for prizss, &amp;c. Tea, consisting of buns and other cakes, was served during the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
