      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>WARRINGTON WALKING DAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>THE PIWCESSIONS.</p>
        <p>On the whole fine weather favoured the annual festival of Sunday School scholars last Fri- day morning. During the week the weather became very unpromising, and the showers of Wednesday and Thursday gave rise to fears of rain on the day of the popular fete. The morning opened rather dull, but towards noon the sun shone forth with great power: Later in the afternoon, however, rain fell for about an hour.</p>
        <p>The streets as on previous occasions were gaily decorated. The work commenced on )[ Thursday, and on Friday morning the scene was ) very brilliant, the whole length of the line of procession being bedecked with bunting. By nine o'clock the public began to assemble along the line of the route of the procession, and when the scholars walked along the streets, the whole presented an animated appearance. The schools commenced to arrive on the plot of ground in front of the Town Hall shortly before ten o'clock, entering by the east gate. It was nearly 10.30 before the procession filed out through the central arch headed by the glue Coat School, whose banner reminded the on-lookers that the school was founaed so far back as 1677. The route taken was through Sankey-st., Arpley-et., Wilson Patten-st., past Arpley Station, Bridge-st., Buttermarket-st., Church-st., after which the children separated for their various churches where services were held. The Blue Coat School was headed by their brass band strider the conductorship of Mr. Rose. After them followed the children at the Workhouse and Padgate schools, displaying a profusion of Union Jacks. They were headed by the Padgate Industrial Schools Brass Band. In the Droeession there were a number of Guardians and officials, amongst whom we noticed Mr. Davies (chairman), Mr. Pilling (vice-chairman), Mrs. Clay, Messrs. A. Jolley, Malkin, Cross, William Houghton, Mr. and Mr:). Mines (Padgate Schools), Mr. and Mrs. Ward (Workhouse), and Mr. S. Sutton (deputy clerk). The Parish Church school children followed, and at their head was the fine brass band of the Bth and 40th Regiments stationed at Orford Barracks. Succeeding them were the members of the Church Lads' Brigade, who presented a smart appearance. Immediately following the large silk banner,there walked the Rev. F. W. Willis (rector), accompanied by Mr. Walton and Mr. Gill. The banners carried by these children were only of a tu9dio- cre description, and in some instances looked positively shabby. The S. George's Mission was headed by the Volunteer Bugle Band under the conductorship of Brigade Drum-Major Plinston. The clergy at the head of the school were the Rev. G. B. Thurston and the Rev. E. P. Wethered, M.A., vicar of Hillinfare. Banket. Mission Sunday school succeeded them in the procession, the Rev. A. A. Markham walking in the forefront. An interesting feature of this school's display was the pretty sight of a number of little girls attired in white and carrying baskets of choice flowers. The S. Paul's brass band headed the scholars attend- ing the Sunday schools in connection with S. Paul's 'Church. Immediately behind the large banner there walked the Ray. H. V. Plant (vicar), Mr. Chantler and Mr. Craddock (churchvfardens). Mr. A. J. Jolley and Miss Westwell were also present. The scholars attend- ing All Saints' Mission ,with the Rev. H.Brierloy (curate) followed next. Holy Trinity School with the Walton brass band at their head succeeded them, and there were also in the procession the Rev. E. C. E Carleton (vicar), Messrs. Forshawi Brierley, John Halton, and Randall. At the head of the Factory-yard Mission there n ere Mr. Westbrook, Mrs. Carleton, and Mrs. Hunt. St. Luke'efeChurch Sunday School followed with the Rev. H. R. Wild. Mr. Pickston and Mr. Peers in attendance. The band of the Ist Volunteer Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment preceded the scholars attending S. James Sunday School. This school, from a picturesque standpoint, outshone the other echeols in the procession. In previous years men- tion has been made of the tine display made by S. James', and this year it more than maintained its reputation. An artistic banner in the form of a shield was in the place of honour, and behind walked the Rev. F. Slater (vicar), Rev. W. D. Sprowle (curate), Mr. Warburton (superintendent), and Mrs. Herbert Woods. Some beautiful banners were carried, and from them a number of coloured ribbons were suspended and these were carried by prettily attired girls. A very conspicuous feature was the mail carts upon which children were drawn. Various flowers had been entwined round the spokes of the wheels, aed above them were canopies of art muslin of various colours. These made a very agreeable change to the procession. Ano her feature, which greatly added to the attractiveness, was the method of walking in procession. The banners, mail carts, lurries. and attendants proceeded in the centre, and a string of scholars walked on either side. St. Ann's Church came next, accompanied by a brass band. At the head of the procession were the Rev. J. Davies, and Mr. F. Taylor. and there were also present the Rev. P. J. Byrne and Miss Davies. The scholars from St. Barnabas Church were in charge of the Rev. G. Allton, and headed by Fletcher, Russell and Company's band. Amongst those in the procession were Messrs. Towers, Princep, and Billinge. and Mrs. Allton was in charge of the members of St. Barnabas Band of Hone. There were several noticeable mail-carts and bassinettes covered with flowers in the procession of St. Peter's school which was headed by Buckley's band. Those connected with the Church Lads' Brigade suc- ceeded them. Amongst those present were the Rev. B. Percival (vicar), Rev. S. J. Fleming (curate), Messrs. John Roberts, Joseph Balreford, James Taylor, Stanley, Knight and H. Taylor (police court missionary). There was a plentiful supply of banners with the Emmanuel (Reformed Church of England) Sunday school at the hea 1 of which there were the Rev. E. T. Reed, Mr. Lees, and Mr. Allen. The rear was brought up by the mem- bers of the Emmanuel Band of Hope. One of the ban- ners was in the form ef a monogram with words "C.E." which had been skilfully worked in flowers. A slight hitch occurred whilst this school was going out of the Park. As in previous years, the Primi- tive Methodist Sunday school were expecting to follow the Emmanuel Church, and were preparing to do so, when one of the marshalls gave the signal to those connected with Buckley-street school to follow on. The officials of the Primitive Methodist school rightly protested against this unusual decision, but it appears that those r.eponsible for the order of the procession had decided that Buckley-street should follow on and they did so. They were accompanied by their own band, and near the head was a daintily decor- ated mail-cart, covered with cotton wool. The Primi- tive Methodists who had a combined procession of the scholars at Legh-st., Sharp-et. and Latchford schools, were headed by the Post Office Band. Amongst those present were the Revs. W. J. Kirkland, Rev. W. E.Lead (Widnes), Mr. Whitley (Stockton Heath), Mr. Joseph Tichett, and Mr. John Podmore. The Latchford Primi- tive Methodists were headed by a drum and fife band conducted by Mr. jchn Cross. The Salvation Army with their band followed next,and were in charge of Captain Langdale. They had a great number of followers, and many of the youngsters present seemed to have been picked up in the streets. The Ragged school bronght up the rear of the long procession,and were headed by their drum and fife band' playing " Onward Christian Soldiers." Mr. Skelland and Mr. Evans were in charge. One banner contained the text" In-as-much as ye have done it unto one of the least of these, ye did it unto Me." _</p>
        <p>The Catholics had a separate procession of scholars from their several schools, and presented an imposing appearance. Tile S. Alban's procession was preceded</p>
      </div>
