      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>COCICHEDGE MISSION S S.—Number of scholars on books ; 220 boys, 230 Girls; average attendance; 140 boys, 130 girls; number of teachers: 12 male, 8 female; number of books in library: 200; superintendents: Messrs. Plinston and H. Maybury. The scholars and teachers left the school about 9 o'clock and proceeded to Hill Cliffs where a most enjoyable day was spent. The Wycliffe Cockhedge Band was in attendance.</p>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION. - Number of scholars on books, 544; average attendance, 396 ; number of teachers, 12 male, 9 temale ; superintendents,Mr. Joseph Monks, jun., and Mr. J. Woolle7. Ihe children of the above school walked in procession to Walton Lea at nine o'clock and had dinner and tea on the field. Mr. Crosfield's generosity provided a means of enjoyment which the scholars fully availed themselves of.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S SUNDAY SCHOOL.—NUMber of scholars on books, 600 boys,Boo girls ; number of teachers, 35 male, 52 female ; superintendents, the Rector, the Revs. G. B. Thurston and A. A. Markham, and Mr. Hodgson. After the procession a service was held in the Parish Church. Sports and games were afterwards proceeded with to the enjoyment of the voungsters. ST. JAMES' (LATCHFORD). —Between 400 and 500 scho- lars; minitters, Rev. F. Slater (vicar) and W.D. Sprowle (curate) ; superintendent, Mr. J. W. Warburton. The scholars of S:, James' School also took part in the pro- cession,and afterwards spent an enjoyable day in a field near the brewery, lent by Messrs. Greenall,Whitley, and Co.</p>
        <p>Sr. MARY'S ROMAN CATHOLIC (13IITTERMA.RIEET-ST.)— Number of scholars on books, 392 boys, 374 girls; aver- age attendance, 333 boys, 305 girls: ntimber of teachers 2 male, 14 female ; ministers, Revs. F.W. Sumner,O.S.B. T. B. Clarkson, 0.5.11., M. E. Duggan, 0.5.8. ; superin- tendent, Rev. M. E. Duggan. After the procession in the morning the scholars spent the afternoon in a field in Gorsey-lane, where every kind of amusement was pro- vided for the children. Tea and refreshments were also supplied to them. CHURCH. OF THE SACRED HEART (LIVERPOOL-ROAD).— Average attendance, 47 boys, 49 girls; number of teachers, 2 females; minister, Rev. M. Ryan. These scholars joined St. Alban's and S. Mary's and spent the day in the same fashion. UNION WonKnousE.—Number of scholars on books 23 ; Miss Ward, schoolmistress ; ministers, Rev. H. V. Pigot, chaplain ; and Rev. Father Ryan,Roman Catholic Instructor. Tim day's programme: The children walked with their respective Sunday schools—St. Paul's and Church of the Sacred Heart. In the afternoon they went by train to l'adgate and joined in the sports in the field with the children belonging to Padgate schoolsoreturning home about 8.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>I'ADOATE INDUSTRIAL SCHOOLS.—Number of scholars on books,BB boys; 61 girls ; average attendance,B6 boys, 58 girls ; number of teachers, 2 male. 1 female ; number of books in library. 60 ; minister, Rev. J. J. Langham ; superintendent, Mr. H. M. Mites. The children of the above schools went by train to Warrington at 9 a.m. and joined the procession in the Park. They attended service in the Parish Church, and the afternoo: was spent in a field at Woolstan, kindly placed at their dis- posal by Mr. Joseph Houghton. Football, cricket, and other games were participated in by the children, and tea, buns, sandwiches' nuts and oranges were served to them. Tney returned home about nine o'clock. BLUE COAT Scaoor..—Number of scholars on books, 33 boys, 24 girls; average attendance, 33 boys, 24 girls; number of teachers, 3 male, 1 female; number of books in library, 200; superintendent, Mr. Edward Rose. The Blue Coat School Boys took part in the procession in the morning, !and there was the usual visit from "Old Blues" and friends in the afternoon. Games, Stc., were greatly enjoyed in the school ground. SALVATION ARMY.—Number of scholars on books, 57 boys, 64 girls ; average attendance, 45 boys, 50 girls; number of teachers, 15 ' • minister, Mr. George Langdon. The day's programme: The procession was joined in by the Army scholars who afterwards went on the park lawn. Tea and dinner were provided to about 1,000 persons.</p>
        <p>BANK•STREET MISSION.—Number of scholars On book, 157 boys; 195 girls; number of teachers, 22 ; number of books in the library, 200; minister, Rev. A. A. Mark- ham. The day's programme consisted of Divine Service at 11 a.m., followed by procession to St. Elphin's Park in the afternoon. Here tea was served at 4.30. A happy time was spent in the usual way.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY (CHURCH OF ENOLAND).—Number of scholars on bluks, 300 ; average attendance, 2GO; num- ber of teachers, 9 male, 11 female ; superintendent, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton. the day's programme consisted of joining the procession from Back Park and proceeding round the town. In the afternoon thoy went to Frod- sham and had tea at Mersey View, Overton Hills. A pleasant day was spent by all.</p>
        <p>Sr. LUKE'S, (LIVBRPOOL-ROLD).—Number of scholars on books. 200 boys; 236 girls; avenge attendance, 150 boys ; 200 girls number of teachers 11 male ; 14 female ; minister, Rev. H. R. Wild (curate in charge) ; superintendent, Rev. H. R. Wild. Tip scholars of this school spent the afternoon in a field off Liverpool-road.</p>
        <p>ALL SAINT'S MISSION.—Number of scholars on books 185 bo,s, 220 girls; average attendance, 160 boys. 190 girls; number of teachers, 15 ; ministers, Rev. H. V. Pigot, (vicar), Ray. H. Brierly, curate; superintendent, Mr. P.Sudlow. The scholars walked in processiorkin the morning, and joined St. Paul's in the field and partook of tea with them.</p>
        <p>WRISH•CHAPEL ROSFIELD-STREET). —Number of scholars on books, boys, 80, girls, 40; average attend- ance, boys, 60, girls, 30 ; number of teachers, 4 male, 3 female; minister, Rev. J. E. Jones; superintendent, Mr. John Humphreys. 'Buses engaged from Mr. Connor's, Sankey-street, drove the children of this school to Dun- ham Park. They started at 12 and reached Dunham in good time. Here the afternoon was made full use of for enjoyment. FRIARS GREEN. —Numbor of scholars on books boys 100, girls 90; average attendance, boys, 93 : girls, 77 ; number of teachers' males 9 ; females, 8 ; number of books in library, 375 ; superintendent, Mr. James Evans. The scholars formed in processional order and took the following route from Friar's-green Chapel : Bold-street, Golborne-street, Bewsey-street, Horsemarket-street, Buttermarket-stref-t, Fenne!-street, to Brick-street School. A field had been engaged in Padgate where the scholars spent the day in an enjoy- able manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>AT PENKETIL</head>
        <p>AT PENKETIL</p>
        <p>Friday being the Warrington Walking Day, the occasion was made a general holiday at Penketh. The Mission scholars, the Sankey School children, and Church Lads Brigade joined forces, and formed a pro- cession round the village: After this function the scho- lars adjourned to a field where the proceedings were of a pleasing character. Games were engaged in and several contests held. Tea was served during the after- noon.</p>
        <p>The scholars attending the Wesleyan Sunday School also walked round the village in the morning. The remainder of the day was spent on the Recreation ground where everything was done to make the time fly merrily. Tea was partaken of and after 9 o'clock the proceedings terminated and the scholars returned home. Mr. Athertori is superintendent of the school,Mr. Joseph Smith (sec.), Mr. John Jackson (treas.), and the Rev. J. Gunnell (the resident minister).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
