      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>Ttip ANNUAL WARRINGTuN WORKS'</p>
        <p>TRIPS.</p>
        <p>Walk IV4rti lag day over, the general desire of the public of fisidagthn was to iva.ve the town as soon as possible for thttl: atld Pastures new." The latter phrase is only eski guratively as the "fields and pastures" were th? shovai, and did not even come under the category ilgr hew," for cut of the total number of trips 10e B?: 'fle town on Saturday morning there were seven akPohl, and it will be generally conceded that a th 4tbit',"ete is no novelty for a large portion of the in- pros,„7„nt,s of this good old town. MI these trips were by various works in the town, those sending Ititlß7„nkPeople to Blackpool being Messrs. Armitage Messrs, M mks, Hall, and Co., the Castle Co. Limited. the Whitecross Co. Limited,Messrs. Wieaalmehd and Co., the Firth Co. Limited, and Sankey also '4IIIE- Toe 0 loreliows of the different local lodges 14,8 combined and paid a visit to Llangollen, kieera*, Crosfield and Sons' employees 01:1Cil tithe, PUrneyed to Llandudno, and in all isayalrections people were leaving by the two ran- kest their favourite pleasure resorts. Naturally, all • auslnds of trippers gave the officials of the two ki emspanies, the L. and N.-W. and the C.L.C., kteittglaTchse addition to their work. Special arrange- -114.'1;10, how,ver, been made and for a general,holi- -Ikt,, tit`ue accommodation ;,,nd train s-rvice was of such a 4ta :_as to be a credit to these Companies. In addi- the excursions from Warrington there was a Van stream of traffic from other industrial centres, Stag People to the seaside,and here too the prepon- Nt e was in favour of Blackpool. At this favourite tee, ,there was a huge crowd and a grand harvest was d'artth by the shopkeep-4rs, etc. In the early part of the ,Ititie.,e weather was not of the pleasantest, for a strong ‘4llofevailed and in the afternoon rain fell for about ,▪ ,lr• After this the sun resumed its ascendancy and resk7 'mat touch to the enjoyment of the visitors by the atmospherical conditions faultless.</p>
        <p>SANKEY WIRE MILLS. tOto.o?ees, relatiyesolid friends from the above Mills, ttotic„rlartibf,r ot 600:left Warrington at seven o'clock .410.'18 Bank Qlay Station, for Blackpool, which they %on' reacheti at a quarter to ten. After spending a fiteellislYable day in viewing the various interesting I_Natterd other places of public entertainment they lag 8h their homeward journey at ten o'clock, arriv- Warrington at 12.30. ?rah, MESSRS. ARMITAGE AND RIGBY. 4cl4,Cockhedge Mills upwards of 1,000 workpeople %eol'ir friends journeyed to Blackpool, and tormed 14. timtbe most successful excursions t,eld in connection rr en-78 Mills for years past. At tne sea-side facilities tiinent were numberless and largely availed of It,l„tetla-IPPers. A good number took advantage of the 0-;:8 which their railway ticket allowed, and stayed -4tf -cuaY night. All the arrangements proved satis- St-triatid reflected credit upon the acting promoters D. Messrs. M. Massey, W. Warburton, and T. CAtrr, 4t RUBBER CO. AND THE FIRTH CO. A L11118:1'5' successful trip was promoted by these conjiintly and under its auspices some -.`11)12.160•4. friends, etc., left Bank Quay ~viatl).-;„ at 6 a.m. en route for the favourite ‘.lsg place. They arrived at their destination about sa', and then had the whole day before them. Needless Ittitrlrt th time was made full use of for enjoyrsent,and Itttala It was found at the numerous places of en- this exe"u' which Blackpool boasts. In connection with 'Sprat io.irs'c'n also, there was no occurrence to mar the t• the and its success reflects credit upon gentleman who had the charge of</p>
