      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER TRIP.—On Saturday a trip in connection with St. Thomas's Church to.k Mace, when a party went at an early hour in 'buses provided by Mr. Edward Connor, to Chester. On reaching the city the party visited the CAthedral and other places of interest, and after partaking of dinner at Mr. Fletcher's, the Rev. S.P. Gray (curate-in-charge) addressed a few words to those present. The party then journed to Eccleston Ferry by Nat, and thence walked to the Duke's gardens, which they viewed with admiration. They next returned to Chester and had tea, after which votes of thanks were passed to the Rev. S. P. Gray, Mr. R. Davies,and others. Those members of the committee who journeyed with the party are deservine, of great praise, inasmuch as they exerted themselves to the utmost in adding to the pleasure of the excursionists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>APPLETON PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>APPLETON PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>A meetine of this committee was held at the London Bridee Inn, Stockton Heath, on Monday evening. Mr. J. Warburton presided. and there were also present, Messrs. F. Pearson, E. Pemberton. Ashton Hayes, H. Eyes, N.orcott, J. Ashton (clerk), J. Swinton (surveyor), and J. Farrington (nuisance inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CORRESPONDENCE ON NIIISA.NCES,</head>
        <p>CORRESPONDENCE ON NIIISA.NCES,</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Clerk to the Cheshire Count' Council with reference to the old river bed at Wilderspool. It stated that the County Council had had communication with the Town Clerk of Warrington, and as a result they (the Warrington Corporation) ,were getting tenders for the taking away the sewage of Stock- ton Heath instead of allowing it to flow in the old river, and would so abate the nuisance arising from it. A letter was read from the Town Clerk of Warrington in which he presented the draft agreement for the sewer- age scheme, and asked the committee to pass the plans as he intended to start at once with the scheme. The plans were adopted on the motion of Mr. Ashton Hayes, seconded by Mr. T. Pemberton. A further letter was read from the Gas Department of the Warrington Corporation statintr that thev had re- ceived as intimation from the Manchester Ship Canal Company that they would not he liable for the lighting of lamps on the approaches to their bridges in this dis- trict. They asked to be informed by -return of post whether the Authority was prepared to take over the lamps or whether they must discontinue lighting them for the present.—A short discussion arose, and it was stated by the Clerk that there was a clause in the Act whereby the Ship Canal Company had to light their bridges. It was also mentioned that the Twentvstep Bridge was not lighted properly and eventually the Com- mittee decided to adjourn their decision to the next meeting, and in the meantime the Clerk sheuld write to the Company asking what lamps they were not going to pay for and also to inform them that they were bound to light the approaches to their bridges. With regard to the paving of Bostock-st. and Gaskell- street upon which the residents of those streets had been asked o state whether they would pave the streets themselves in a reasonable manner or have it done by the District Council and be charged with the cost, the Clerk said he had only received replies from three of the inhabitants of those streeta,and they sieriN favourable to the work being done.--Mr. Pemberton, as a prenerty owner in Gaskell-st., said he could speak for the tenants who wished to know what was meant by reasonable re- pair.—Mr.Warburtom asked the same question on behalf of Bostock-st., where he had property.—The c ,mrnittee adjourned this matter to the next meeting in orrh.r that more information m;ght be procured. Mr. Farrington referred to a complaint from Coun- cillor Hepherd about the stench arising from pianure boats staying a long time at London Bridge Wharf. tie said he had informed Mr. Hepherd that a nilo had been put up requiring that the boats most he rreievei after 24 hours from the time of their coming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>FRODSRAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSRAM.</p>
        <p>FREE Cneacn SUNDAY SZ...gOOL.—In connection with the above the anniversary sermons were preached on Sunday by the Rev. H. Perciwil in the Methodist Free Church. In the afternoon the preacher conducte I a well attended special service. Special music and anthems were rendered by the choir assisted by the scholars. The offertoriee realised £l4-. TRAP ACCIDENT —Shortly after ten o'clock on Tuesday night, Mr. Rydd, of the Uplands, was driving down Red Lane to the station for Overton, when his horse took fright. It galloped down the incline all Verr rapid andiMr. Kydd, who jumped out, was sevN-ely injure 4 about the bands and feet. Mr. Haseiton, Earlestown, who was also in the phaeton was thrown out, and susi-ained fractures in both ribs. be ,ides bPirc severely bruised and cut. The animal was store, the (4s-</p>
        <p>Holly!Bund, where it ran against the gate P'` upants of the phaetor have sinco been under the care cf Drs. Selby and Burton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, JULY 11, 1896.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRTINEENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Town Hall on Saturday morning, before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, James Sharpies, collier, of Haydock, was charged with being drunk in Market- street, Earlestown, the previous night,and was fined 2. 6d. and costs. VIADUCT INSTITUTE TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The results of the Science and Art examinations in Machine Construction and Drawing, and Mathe- matics held in connection with the above classes, are as follows :—Machine Construction and Draw- ing : W. 0. Johnson, A Ist; John Martin, A Ist; F. P. Houghton, A 2nd; W. H. Beilby, J. A. Bullock, R. F. S. Campbell, T. C. Higgins, A. P. Hill, F. P. Manning, C. Partington, B. pass; J. A. Chambers,H. Chandley, A. Harrison, G. A. Harrison, D. Morris, D, Prescot, J. F. Reade, H. Williamson, E fair ; mathe- matics, stages 1, 2 and 3, J. E. Benison. stage 2, 2nd; D. Fazakerley, M. E. Gibbon, A. Lyons, stage 1, pass; S. M. Baybut, G. Burgess, S. Cook, T. Cook, T. H. Gee, G. Goff, B. Elewitt, F. E. Weeks, stage 1, f air.</p>
        <p>VIADUCT INSTITUTE TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The results of the examination in physiography, held under the Science and Art Institute in connec- tion with the above classes, are as follows :—S. M. Baybut and IL W. Nicholson, E pass. M. E. Leather, H. J. Price and M. Sweetlove. E, fair. STRANGE FIND AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Some extensive alterations are in course of pro- gress in connection with the District Schools, Earlestown. The other day whilst getting out the foundations for the building, the men employed upon the work, came across a large space of soft mud, and observing something moving amongst the mud, they made search and dug out two large eels, each measuring 2ft. 6in. long. They were both very lively and in good condition, and were discovered at a depth of 10ft. It appears that the site is upon an old pit, which has, however,been filled up for over 33 years. If this is the case the eels must have been embeddied in the mud that length of time, and it is inconceivable that they should be alive and in a healthy condition.</p>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>An ordinary monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown on Monday afternoon, when Mr. C. B. F. Borr•n, J.P., C.C. presided, and there were also present—Messrs. J. %V. Emmett, P. McMahon, W. Gilmour, F. J. Wood, J. Simpson, J.Slee, W. Beatty, E. T. Evans, T. Ormand, W. Collingwood, the Rev. E. W. %V. Kaye, J. Worsley (law clerk), W. W. Shirley (clerk) and R. Brierley (surveyor). MR. GARRATT RONAYNE.</p>
        <p>Before the passing of the minutes of the previous meet- ing, Mr. Beatty asked on whose. authority Mr. Garratt Ronavne had been imprisoned.—The Chairman What clause is that on the minutes P—Mr. McMahon : It should be on the Highways, etc. committee's minutes.— The Chairman then ruled the matter out of order and the minutes were carried.</p>
        <p>Dutton,Earlestown. He should be pleased if next time Mr. Gilmour would point out the names which were omitted so that they might be asked for a quotation.— The minutes were then confirmed.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the above committee were adopted on the motion of Mr. Ormand, seconded by Mr. Coiling- wood.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved the passing of these minutes. Mr. McMahon seconded, and it was carried.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL MOTION.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved that the following resolution pasqed by the Commissioners on the 16th day of Novem- ber,lB93, viz. :—" That tte list of charges and regula- tions for the letting of the Town Hall now brought for- ward be approved" be rescinded.—Mr. McMahon seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>The Chairman also gave notice that at the next meet- ing he intended to propose the re-appointment of Dr. Watkins as Medical Officer of Health on the same terms and conditions as before.</p>
        <p>THE PUBLICATION OF THE MINUTES BEFORE CONFIRMATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour said sometime ago it was decided that the Press should be provided with a copy of the minutes beforehand. It was then thought that they would not be used before confirmeion and he now thought they ought not to be printed before the meeting.—Mr. Emmett said he was of the same opinion. He thought it would be bet- ter if the minutes were not printed and circulated prior to:the Council meeting. Sometimes they were altered.— Mr. Gilmour : I give it as a matter to come before the next committee meeting. This concluded the business and the Council rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>RUMMAGE SALE AT LIAYDOCK.</head>
        <p>RUMMAGE SALE AT LIAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>In aid of the building debit of £350 on St. James' Church, Haydock, a two day's rummage sale was held in the Haydock National Schools on Friday. and Saturday. The articles which included those left over from the last bazaar, and others contri- buted by friends,were displayed on one large stall, and consisted of a variety of things. The sale was opened by Mrs. Street, of Hal dock Lodge, and the attendance on both days was very fair. Entertain- ments were given by the St. James' Amateur Ministrels, and by the teachers, scholars, &amp;c., of the schools. On Friday night the Haydock brass band was present, and played several selections of music. The proceeds of the two days' sale amounted to £4O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARS.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARS.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. G. Clarke pre- sided, and there was a very good attendance of members. Two candidates were admitted to mem- bership, and several items of business were trans- acted. The evening's programme was a flower ser- vice, for which Bro. Nicholson (secretary) was responsible. There was a splendid selection of beautiful flowers, and during the evening a number of songs and readings were given by Bros. Clarke, and Warburton, and Sisters Hatton, Jackson, and Clarke. Sister F. Holliday accompanied. The flowers at the close of the meeting were taken to the Maydock Cottage Hospital for the benefit of the patients. GOOD TEMPLARY.—EBENEZER LODGE. _ _</p>
        <p>The Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session in Co-operative Hall, Earle-street, on Tues- day evening. Bro. T. Dean, C.T., presided. The Secretary read the digest of No. 1 Convention to be held at St. Helens on Saturday the 11th inst. Bro. A. Morris reported on the demonstration, and it was decided to have an open meeting on the visit of the Leigh Lodge on Tuesday evening next. Greet- ings were received and accepted from Mission Lodge, Warrington. The following members took part in the programme of the evening :—Bro. Dean, a stump speech; Bro. Brown, solo; Bro. Kenyon, recitation ; Sister Whitty solo ; Sister Owens,:solo ; Sister Ord. recitation, Sister Woods, solo. Thanks were tentered to those taking part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO A WORRIEA.N.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO A WORRIEA.N.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at noon the men employed in the wood machine shop of the Viaduct Works, Earles- town, assembled for the purpose of presenting Mr. John Mitchell, who for many years worked amongst them, with a token of their respect and esteem. Mr. Mitchell who is now becoming advanced in years, was for a very long period engaged in the machine shop, and he has found it necessary to apply for a transfer to another department in the works. He is much respected by the men, and as a consequence they decided to mark the separation by presenting him with a splendid silver albert and pendant. The presentation was Made by Mr. W. Gilmour on behalf of the men, and in the course of his remarks he referred in a feeling manner to Mr. Nlitchell's connection amongst them. Mr. Mitchell suitably acknowledged the gift, which came as a surprise upon him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>IMPUDENT ROBBERY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>IMPUDENT ROBBERY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Saturday morn- ing before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, four young men, whose ages range from 19 to 24 years' named Patrick Woods, Fairclough-st. ; Miehael Gibbons, Legh-st.; William Shurrock, Regent-st. ; and Peter Brown, Derby-st., were charged with stealing 354. in silver and two tobacco boxes from the house, 49, Railway Bow, Vitriol Square, Earlestown, on the 2nd inst., the property of John Walsh, an army pensioner. It appears that Walsh keeps a shop, and dispenses ginger beer, &amp;c., and his place is much frequented by young men. On the evening in ques- tion, prisoners were amongst the customers, and it is alleged the prisoner Wood went into prosecutor's bedroom, and took the boxes vferred to, whilst Gibbons put part of a cigarette into a glass of beer which was afterwards drunk by prosecutor. He then became drowsy,and Shurrock held him whilst Brown put his hand into his trousers pocket and took the money. They then left the house and divided the money. They were remanded until next Saturday to Newton Petty Sessions. EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHURCH CHOIR. The annual picnic in connection with the above choir, took place on Saturday. The party number- ing about 30, including the Rev. .T. Bartley left Earlestown by the 7.20 train, a carriage being re- served for their convenience. Windermere was the destination, but the train being nearly an hour late the party proceeded at once to Beetle's where an excellent dinner was provided, and thoroughly en- joyed. After dinner the party embarked on the terry steamer for a trip to Ambleside. On the way however, rain began to fall, and continued to do so for the greater part oi the afternoon and consider- ably interfered with the party's enjoyment. About four o'clock however it cleared off, and the party after having tea, drove back to Windermere, and ascended Orrest Head, from which a splendid view of the lake and surrounding country was obtained. A start was made for home about six o'clock,Earles- town being reached &amp;lout 10 o'clock. The arrange- ments. which were of an excellent character, were carried out by Mr. Bradley. PRESENTATIONS TO SCHOOL TEACHERS AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The Earlestown District School closed on Friday last for the slimmer holidays which will extend over a period of five weeks. This is longer than usual, owing to structural improvements being made in the school. Two spacious class rooms are being added, which will accommodate an additional 200 children. On Friday afternoon before closing the school, an interesting event took place, Miss Howarth, and Miss A. Molyneux, two of the teachers, being recipients of presents at the hands of their colleagues,and the children. Miss Howarth has been a teacher at the school since it became a mixed school, and during that time she has gained high esteem for the good work she has carried on. In recognition of her labour she war presented with a gold bangle. Miss A. Molyneux, who has served her apprenticeship and leaves to complete her training at the Home and Colonial College,London, has also done good work at the school, and in ac- knowledgement she was presented with a writing casP, on which was a suitable inscription. Mr. Brown, headmaster, at the presentation offered a few suitable words of introduction with regard to the respective teachers, and the presontation was made by two of the scholars. The managers have also presented Miss Howarth with a beautifully bound volume of Tennyson's poems, end Miss Moly- neux with a volume of Wordsworth' poems. WAGGONETTE ACCIDENT AT VULCAN. On Sunday afternoon Mr. Albert Boughey, of Tamworth-street, Earlestown, hired a waggonette and horse for tie purpose of driving with several other pe-sons to Lynam. Wheat loaded the vehical contained six a;dults and two chi:dren, and they left Earlestown ~atiout half-past two. All went well until passing under Wargrave-road bridge,when the horse shied,and the waggonette coming in contact with the curb stone was nearly up,et. Mr. Boughey was driving and he managed to get the horse under control. On reaching the Val( In brow however, the horse again became restive an I finally bolted. The waggonette swayed from side to side and ultimately turned over, pitching all the occu- pants out violently. Mrs. Boughey was most seri- ously hurt, she having sustained a fractured shoulder blade. She was at the time nursing h baby, six months old, but strange to say the infant e,,capecl without a scratch. A man named Beau- mont was severely cut about the face, and a young woman was ery badly shaken, and sustained a slight con:;ussion of the brain, in consequence of which sLe has been confined to her bed. Mr. Boughey escaped with a slight sprain to one wrist, ! hut the other occupants with the exception of a few sgratches escaped without injury. The vehicle j was very badly damaged ; both lamps were broken, I the front and sides and splashers were smashed,and one spring damaged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee were adopted on the motion of the Rev. E. W. W. KAye seconded by Mr. Gilmour.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.—AIR. MCDIAIION AND THE " ENCLOSURES."—A BREEZE. Mr. Wood moved the adoption of the minutes of the above committee with the exception of minute 145 which he suggested should read "That on any day dur- ing ordinary school hours the officer of the Council shall call at any school and take from the registers the names of absentees and irregulars, if such information is not sent to him in the usual course." Hp then referred to the letters which had passed between the Council, the managers' correspondent of St. Mary's Schools (Father Lennon), and the Education Department. One letter from the Rev. J. Lennon stated that the committee had no authority to demand that any of the Council's officers should have a right to enter any public element- ary school of the district at any hour, or on any school business. In reply t 3 that, he (Mr. Wood) had a letter from the Education Department which said "That the documents required by the Elementary Education Act must be produced at any reasonable time they may be demanded." The Council thus had the authority and their officers had the right to inspect the registers at any reasonable time, during school hours. If that was objectionable to the school managers they had the alt?rnative of sending a list, ,which was done by every school but St. Mary's. In the letter they were credited with improving the con- dition of St. Mary's school in the estimation of the Eincation department. He (Mr. Wood) was pleased their efforts in that direction had borne some fruit. 'There was also another letter sent by the managers of St. Mary's to the Education Department. That letter was an extraordinary document, remarkable for its inaccu- racies and misstatements and altogether unworthy of the source from which it emanated. The first pargraph went over very much the same ground as the letter he had answered, but the portion having reference to the School Attendance Officer appeared to be of such a libellous nature that had it teen addressed to any member of the committee it would have needed being brought before a jury to be settled, and he had no doubt whatever that substantial damages would be awarded, they had their duty to their officers as well as to the public and speaking from his own per- sonal knowledge of the School Attendance Officer, Mr. Ball,he could say that the position he had held in a large firm would never have been his had there been any suspicion of untrustworthiness in him. He would not have held that position for a day. He held a position where trustworthiness was an essential point, for 28 years. In the speaker's capacity as chairman of the School Attendance Committee, he had been a good deal to Mr. Ball's office,and at all times of the day,and he had never once found him intoxicated or "sleeping off the effects "of potations. He had been always sober,oblig. ing, and in every way anxious to aceede to the wishes of the committee. It was astonish- ing that such a letter. so recklessly compiled, should be sent to the Elucation Department. He concluded that the writers considered the end justifie d the means as they said that no census of the children had been taken in that district since 1870, by the com- mittee. They might not be surprised to learn that a census was taken in 1877 and another in 1883. With regard to the prosecutions—it being alleged that there had not been as many as a dezen prosecutions in 23 year., for even the grossest and most flagrant violations of the law's requirements—that was equally false. There had beer,, as a matter of fact, 137 prosecutions from March 1878 to November 1895. That should satisfy the overbearing policy for wholesale prosecution advocated by St. Marc's School. With reference to the 15 cases of habitual absentees, they had already been disposed of to the satisfaction of the whole of the com- mittee. One paragraph he was bound to admit 'was cerrect, and that was that he (Mr. Wood) as chairman of the committee, stated that the School Attendance officers' report was entirely satisfactory. He still contended that it was, and the course he adopted at the time to satisfy himself was the course he should take again. They were accused in thelet- tors of holding caucus meetings on the subject. That was hardly worth answering or denying. It was somewhat singular too that on the last letter there was marked "Four enclosnrea,":and when this wee; pointed out in Committee that Mr. McMahon should offer to pro 'uce the' enclosures from his pocket----Mr. MaMahon : I deny it entirely.—Mr. Wood : Those people who make such charges should see that their own acts are above suspicion. Continuing, he said that all through the controversy he had made it a point that everything should be perfectly straightforward, and published in tho press. (Hear, hear.) As they had the right for their officer to attend at any school and take what he required from the registers providing that such a list of requirements was not sent, he begged to put for- ward his motion on minute 145.—Mr. McMahon : lam not the authority for those letters, therefore I am not aecountahle for them. I again deny Mr. Wood's accusa- tion against mrself.--The Clerk : The words "Four enclosures" were written on Father Lennon's letter.— Mr. McMahon : That meant that there were four enclos- ures sent to the Education Department hut not four enclosure? in the letter to this Board. So far as the Chairman was concerned he thought he would find that the managers of St. Mary's school had tak,n care that their actions were above board.—Mr. Gilmour: Whether Mr. McMahon remembers it or not, I solemnly declare that the chairman said " There are four enclosures. I can only find two." Mr. McMahon then attempted to bring them out of his pocket, when it was suggested that Father Lennin should be written to about the enclosures. There are other members of the Board here, who were present, and can state what is correct.—Mr. Wood : It is as Mr. Gilmour says. I looked for the enclosures, and Mr. McMahon said, " If you want the others--Mr. McMahon (interrupting) : Nothing of the sort.—Mr. Wood (continuing) : fie said "If you want them, they are here."—The Rev.E. Kaye : I support Mr. Gilmour in what he says.—Mr. Emmett said that when he came to read the letters he knew the statements about the census were incorrect, and they astonished him. How Mr. McMahon could agree with Father Lennon's statements was beyond his eomprehension,es he must know. being,Le (Mr. Emmett thought, a member of the Commissioners when the cen- sus was taken.—Mr. McMahon said he did not think he was.—The Chairman said the account that had been given by Mr. Gilmour was quite in accordance with what was in his memory. Mr.McMahon certainly offered to produce the enclosures. He thought they could con- gratulate the Chairman of the Committee (Mr. Wood) for having during the correspondence kept his tamper, and con-hinted it in a gentlemanly and business-like manner. Speaking as a manager and correspondent of a school, he was pleased to have the matter clearly brought before them and clearly de- cided by the Department. They were told that as managers they were bound to furnish the Council with full particulars of absentees. They were bound to allow the officer of the Council to go into the schools and take those particulars during the ordinary business hours. It was satisfactory to know that the matter had been clearly decided by the Department and the con- tention settled. He hoped that all managers of the schools would assist the Council, which had a duty to perform to the public. BO that, the education of the district might be furthered and not retarded.—Mr. Ormand seconded the adoption of the minutes as amended, and they'were carried. . lECHN,CAL INSTRUCTION AND HIGHWAYS COMMITTEES. The minutes of these committees were confirmed without discussion.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.---A 4IIESTIDN OF TENDERS. The Chairman moved that the minutes of the above committee be confirmed.—Mr. Simpson seconded. Gilmour referred to the quotations for the supply of gas brackets, etc. He found thats there were makers of such appliances in the town and neigh- bourhood who had never been asked to: tender. It was not fair to ask several gentlemen for quotations without giving the other manufacturers the same opportunities. He suggefted that in future they should give all the manufacturrrs in the town a chance of competing. —The Chairman said he quite azreed with what Mr. Gilmour had said. A number of persons were asked to tender and thacommittee accepted the tender of Mr. J. H.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WARRINGTW COILN TY CGURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTW COILN TY CGURT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. THE CLAIM AGAINST A NEWTON PUBLICAN.—RaIph Johnson, cabinet maker and upholsterer, Warrington, claimed from John William Webster, licensee of the Millstone Inn, Newton. £7 ss. 61 for veterinary attend- ance upon and physic for his horse in consequence of in- juries it had sustained by being wilfully driven into on the highroad between Winwick and Warrington on the 24th February, and for horse hire by plaintiff during the time his own horse was unfit to be worked ic conse- quence of the injuries it received.—Mr. Fowler (Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.) repreeented the plaintiff. and Mr. Stuart, solicitor, appeared on behalf of the defend- ant. The case was adjourned from the previous Court partly heard. Only one more witness was called.—John Millington, tailor, 27, High-street, Newton, stated that he was with Webster on the 24th February at the time of the occurrence. He remembered seeing Mr. Johnson's trap. It was about five yards off when they saw it. It was a very dark night and they heard it before they saw it. Mr. Herbert shouted and Mr. Webster whistled. The trap was on top of them before they knew where they where. They did not pull across the road. The plaintiff never made any complaint that his horse was hurt.—ln cress-examination, witness said they only had two drinks, one at the Eagle and Child and the other at the Old Ball. They were about two yards from the fence when the collision took place. There was plenty of room for a vehicle to pass.—His Honour said the de- fendant was put out of Court by the fact that the plain- tiff's horse was damaseed on the near shoulder. If the defendant was on his own side the accident never could have happened. How Could the injury have come about it it was not a collision by the defendant? It was a pity that the witnesses for the defence could not be dealt with for committing perjury.fle believed they had committed gross perjury. He would give judgment for the plain- tiff for £7 and costs. A BREACH OF CONTRACT AND ITS CONSEQUENCES.--.- ; Joseph Buckley, of Bewsey-road, Warrington, made a I claim of £3 3s. for breach of contract against the Bifur- cated Rivet Company of Warrington,on the sale of seven tons of scrap steel sawings.—Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, appeared on behalf of the plaintiff, and Mr. P. Davies, defended.—Mr. A. Browee, in stating the case, pointed out that the plaintiff had had five transactions with the defendant company with regard to the purchase of scrap I steel sawings at 225. a ton. In April last, Mr. Beesley came to the plaintiff and told him that there was a quantity of the sawings at the Bifurcated Rivet Works and that he could have them at the old price. The nla,!ntiff agreed to purchase the material, and afterwards his father agreed to re-sell it to the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Ccmpany, Limited. In the mean- time a representative of the defendant firm came from London, and on aseertaining that the material was sold to Mesers. Pearson and Knowles, Limited, he went to the firm .direct,and sold the material to them over the head of the plaintiff who had already purchased it.—Joseph Buckley, book-keeper at the Whitecross Works, living at 26, Bewsey-road, stated that he paid 22a. a ton for the sawings to the defendant company in the five previous transactions. On Friday, May Bth,Mr. Beesley, the manager of the Bifurcated Rivet Company came to him at Whitecross works, and said he could "take that stuff at your old price." Witness said he would take it, and told him whsn he would remove it. Witness took a sample and gave his father instructions to sell the material. He went to the defendant com- pany's works on the following Monday to tell them that he was going to remove it in a week's time. Mr. Beesley then told him that the managing director had been down, and on hearing where the scrap was sent to said " Here goes to Pearson and Knowles direct." He did so and Mr. Beesley told him that he had got 30s. a ten r it. Witness told him that he had bought the scrap, and if they allowed an ounce to be removed he would claim his margin of profit. The scrap weighed seven tons and was removed. His claim represented the amount of profit he would have received had he sold it.—ln cross-examination witness admitted that he told Mr. Beesley that it was pretty sharp practice on the part Af Mr. Jocelyn to sell the scrap to Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. He denied that Mr. Beesley told him that the scrap wPs sold to Mr. Forshaw.—Mr. Ge,arge Buckley, sen., patent gas maker at Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, stated that in April last he went to see Mr. Beesley at the works so that he might judge of the quantity of material to be removed. His son had brought him a sample, and he had sold the stuff to Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. He made the bargain with Mr. Morris, the manager. Beesley told him that Mr.Forshaw wanted .a load, and witness said he would be quite willing to waive his claim of it.—For the defence, Mr.Da,vies,after remarking on the fact that the plaintiff took a sample of the scrap, which, he contended showed that no definite contract had been concluded, he pointed out that the defendant's manager did not tell the plaintiff that he could have the scrap at all. What Mr. Beesley did say to the plaintiff was. "Mr. Forshaw has not sent for that stuff yet, and it is pos- sible that you may he able to get it in the usual wa,v."—Alfred Beesler, manager of the Bifurcated Rivet Works, residing at 107, Lovely-lane, stated that when the plaintiff asked him about taking the scrap he told him that thev were going to part with it to Mr. Forshaw for 255. a ten. He never offered the scrap for sale to the plaintiff, nor did the plaintiff tell him that if he sold it he would sue for his margin of pro- fit.—ln cross-examination, witness admitted that Mr. Buckley was the only person who had bought the scrap at the works.—His Honour said he must give judgment for the plaintiff. The very fact that the plain- tiff took a sample of the scrap after Mr. Forshaw had declined to pnrehase it, was a strong piece of evidence that the plaintiff had bought•the scrap and it was his. He took this second sample, not for the purpose of see- ing whether he w,uld buy it. but for the purpose of re- selling it. He (his Honour) must come to the conclusion that a contract had been made, and that the plaintiff had a right to complain of the non-completion of that contract by the defendants. There was no doubt that there had been an actual sale nPentiatpri with Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. He would all aw £3 3s, damages.</p>
        <p>WINNINGTON PARK RECREA i'ION CLUB v. WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB. The Winnington Park Recreation Club journeyed to Warrington on Saturday to play their first fixture this season with the Liberal Club, Bewsey-atreet. Score : WINXINGTON PARE. LIBERAL CLUB. 11-15 up. 11-15 up. Sing. D'ble. Sing. D'bie. j Naylor (captain) 3 1 8 J gankey... . ......... 11 118 A J Robinson ll f B•Shearer ............ 7 1 A R Starkey ll} 2 A Jones 15 J Abbott 1 R Anderton ......... 11 1 William Hughes T B Burgess W Unsworth ll 1 12j Bennion Henry Reeves , Thomas whitby ... 9 i , James Savage William Norc oss.. 11 r ' Thomas Craddock 6J " C Stringfellow Thomas aordon .„ W Oakes 2 1 ~ S Massey ll 1 Thomas Hurst R btringfellow 6 1 ~ M Parkes . James Greenhough 1 j " J Jennings ll 116</p>
        <p>Total 107 62 Total 154 119 169 273 169</p>
        <p>Majority for Warrington 104</p>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL TEACHER'S TRIP.—The teachers of the Wes- leyan Sunday School had their annual trip on Saturday. They were joined by the chapel choir, the combined forces numbering 49. The destination was Castleton, the pretty Derbyshire resort. The party visited the caves in charge of the guide,: and enjoyed the trip im- mensely. Home was reached in gad thne the trip be• lug a signal success.</p>
        <p>Tr An inquest was held at C(Thham liall,Kent,on Monday' on the body of Lady Mary Bligh,aged 28 years,daughter of the Earl of Darnley. The deceased lady,who was said to have been in a state of irritation for a long time was found drowned in a pond in the grounds of the hall on Sunday night. The jury found that she had committed suicide while suffering from temporary insanity. TOI3ACCONIST3 acmnaeneing ; write for Illustrated Guide and Catalogue (259 pages) !d. TOBACCONISTS' OUTFITTING CO.-186, Euston-road, London. Manager, HENRY MYERS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LOCAL CLERICAL APPOINTMENTS.</p>
        <p>At the Registry Office, Liverpool, on Tuesday, the Bishop instituted the Rev. Tames Davies to the benefice of St. Ann's, Warrington, and the Rev. Edmund Peel Wethered, M.A., to the benefice of Hollinf are, near Warrington.</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS.—GARLAND, Bridge-street, War- rington,—Advt. WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Occasional Court on Monday morning, before Mr. William Houghton, the fo:lowing per- sons were fined for being drunk and incapable— Mary Jane Wolstencroft, at Rixton, on the 4th inst., fined 2s. 6d. and costs; Samuel Cooper and Daniel Carnell for being drunk at Poultln on the sth inst., fined 2s. 6d. each and costs.</p>
        <p>EVERY CYCLIST should use JOHN S. GRE EN'S NEW ROAD MAP,6O miles about WarringLon,lB.6d.nett, at 34, Bridge-st. and 23,Buttermarket-st.—Advt. A TREAT FOR WARRINGTON LADS.</p>
        <p>On Monday about 40 poor Warrington lads, who had been selected by Mr. Jones,chief school attend- ance inspector for the borough, at the request of Mr. Lee,of Cockhedge Mills, left Bank Quay station for a week's encampment at Birkdale. Ji orty other poor children will enjoy a similar privilege when the other boys return to Warrington. The departure of the lads on Monday was witnessed by Mr. and Mrs. Ziba Armitage, Mrs. Deacon, and Miss Apple- ton. Similar trips are given annually to Warring- ton lads, and the privilege is much appreciated.</p>
        <p>Mr t PONSONBY'S HAIR HESTOREtc. a great discovery, a Most Refreshing Wash, Marvellous Results, Innumetable Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. In great demand by Professionals, makes the Hair Grow, prevents Bald- ness, Invigorating, restores hair to its natural colour, gives healthy and youthful appearance. Most astonish- ing'results ; 2s 6d Bottle, post free.—Address Mts. PON- SORRY. 25, Lime-st., Liverpool—Advt. MARRIAGE OF THE REV, P. J. BYRNE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the marriage was solemnised of Miss Jessie Madeline Morton, daughter of Mr. Morton, residing at Heaton Chapel, near Manchester, and the Rev. P. J. Byrne, curate of St. Ann's Church, Warrington. The service took place at Christ's Church, West Didsbury, at 10,30 a.m. The officiat- ing clergyman was the Rector, the Rev. W. Thomp- son, assisted by the Rev. J. Davies, of St. Ann's Church, Warrington,and the Rev. D. Morton,brother of the bride. The best man was the Rev. G. Atkin- son, and the bridesmaids were Misses Ethel and May Morton. At the wedding breakfast there were present Mr. and Mrs. Morton (parents nf the bride), the bride and bridegroom, Mr. and Mrs. Taylor, the Rev. W. Thompson, and the Rev. G. Denyer. After the breakfast, which was of a strictly private char- acter, the newly-married pair left for Edinburgh to spend their honeymoon. Tne presents were very numerous and of a costly nature. TRAVELLING TRUNKS.—GARLAND, Ironmonger, Warrington.—Advt. BUCKLEY-STREET SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The members and friends of the Young Men's Bible Class belonging to the above school had their annual picnic on Saturday, the place selected for the day's outing being Beeston Castle. The party, numbering about 60, assembled at the school about seven o'clock, and was conveyed in 'busses to Bees- ton, which was sately reached about eleven o'clock. On arrival at Beeston the party walked to Peck- forton, permission having been given to them to go through the Castle. Having viewed the Castle, tbe party walked back to Beeston and partook of a capital tea, which was thoroughly enjoyed. After tea various games were indulged in by all, on the green of the old castle at Beeston. Towards six o'clock preparations were made for the homeward journey, and the party reached Warrington shortly after ten, after having spent an enjoyable day. The picnic arrangements were admirably carried out by Mr. A. Leech, to whom great praise is due.</p>
