      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BURGLARS' ACTIVITY AT LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>BURGLARS' ACTIVITY AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Between Friday and Saturday, the 3rd and 4th inst., no fewer than four attempts at burglary took place at Latchford. The burglars' first attempt ap- pears to have been about 2 a.m. on the premises of Mr. Thomas Hankey, provision dealer, near the Cheshire Cheese, where the thieves effected an en- trance by cutting out the glass and then taking out the screw fastener of one of the back windows. hile at work upstairs a lady who was staying at the house wakenee, and asked a friend what time it was. This seems to have alarmed the thieves, who made good their escape without the inmates of the house being aware of the presence of their unwelcome visitors. At six o'clock in the morning Mr. Hankey found all the backdoors wide open, although nothing was missed. The thieves then appear to have crossed the road to the back of Councillor Ellison's premises, a dwelling-house and bootshop. There they took out a pane ot the work- shop window, thus gaining admission to a back scullery, the window of which they prized and se- cured access to the kitchen. After collecting and packing several articles ready for removal, they attempted to get into the front part of the house and to the bedrooms. Their way being blocked by a locked door, they procured tools from the work shop and at once took off the lock, but fortunately Mr. Ellison had bolted the door on the opposite side, and the burglars were foiled in this endea- vour. Apparently they then procured a ladder and scaled the roof of the workshop, and set to work forcing the back bedroom window. While engaged in this they were alarmed by Mrs. Ellison getting up to attend to a grandchild who was crying. Mrs. Ellison states that at this time she heard a peculiar scraping sound, which she attributed to the sway- ing of the trees in the garden. Only a pair of boots and Mrs. Ellison's purse, containing about 2s. have been stolen. Wash-lane day schools were entered, during the same night, but nothing was taken, ana also a grocer's lock-up shop in Knutsford-road, from which some groceries were stolen. All the above operations had been carried on much in the same manner, and the police consider the work to be that of tramps. No clue has been afforded towards the capture of the marauders.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. MONKS, FIALL, AND CO. There was an unfortunate mistake made by the rail- way authorities with regard to the excursion train which was to have taken the workmen and friends of Messrs. Monks, Hall, and Co. to Blackpool. It had been timed to leave Bank Quay Station at ten minutes to eight,but the train did not arrive until after ten o'clock. In the meantime, the trippers—or mot of them—took advantage of other excursion trains,apd the party of 500 was split up into various sections,and conveyed to their destination. Many of them came home again at night in a similar manner. The committee who had charge of the arrangements, hut who were in no way responsible for the mistake, consisted of the following :—Messrs. Tonge, Brady, A. Birch, W. Bennett, E. Lloyd, Joseph Elwell, Joseph Adams, and Joseph Eggington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MESSRS. J. CROSFIELD AND SONS.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. J. CROSFIELD AND SONS.</p>
        <p>Once again the employes of this firm with their friends journeyed to Rhyl and Llandudno. The work- men were granted free tickets, and the fares for friends were 33. to Rhyl and 33. 6d to Llandudno. About 600 trippers entrained at Bank Quay Station, and a start was made shortly before six o'clock. Rhvl was reached at 7.30. and a few got out, but the majority of the excur- sionists proceeded to Llandudno, which has been called " The Queen of watering places." The weather on the whole was very favourable, arid the trippers seem to have bPen more fortunate in this respect than the large number of Warringtonians who journeyed North. The principal places of interest were visited during the thirteen hours at the seaside, a start for the " good old town "not being made until 9.45. It was after mid- night when they arrivkd home again. Mr. J. Willet made satisfactory arrangements for the trip.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SHOOTING HOAIING PIGEONS,</head>
        <p>SHOOTING HOAIING PIGEONS,</p>
        <p>A SNIAR I' SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>At the Rowley Regi-i Police Court on Wednesday, a young man named Jos-uh Kinsey, residing in Chapel-st., Tividale, was chard with unlawfully shooting a homing pigeon, valued at 305., the property of Joseph Keay. of Sparkbrook, Itir ningham. Mr. Plant, of Dud- ley, defended. On satiiraav, the 28`11 ult., the com- plainant and other,pigeon fanciers despatched pigeons from London to Bitininham, and when complainant's pigeon was flying ov.r Tividale, defendant was seen to shoot it and wouni ir. Upon defendant being told by Police-constable R aiiiison that he had shot the pigeon, he said "I hli a gun licence, and while it lasts whatever comes over Iv re. whether they are prize birds or not,l am going to ha v(i.tti, m." A witness stated that men were out shooting ru:!ions shortly after daybreak, and frequently many v,ht,hle birds , were shot down. Mr.Bassano, the presidiw, rual;istrate,said frequent com- plaints were made of valh:thie birds being shot down by such fellows as the deftrl(t , and he bad no hesitation in saying that such c ws disgraceful and selfish. If the law htd permirs p.‘nuld have had great plea- sure c,cifs. Ile would have sure in irfticting a fir.r. to pay the maximum 40,. 10i. damage, and the costs, 165., or m vita' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-07-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>SUICIDE OF LOVERS.,</head>
        <p>SUICIDE OF LOVERS.,</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Taylor. held an inquest on Wedn.egcleelgl;(l the Dewsbury and District General Infirmary, In %raja to the deaths of Florence Kilburn (15) and Berll-410 Brooke (18), by drowning in the Calder and Einef Canal.—John Kilburn, hand-loom weaver, of nt'ar ang Carr, father of the girl,deposed that he saw bisdu,a alive for the last time on Thursday,the 2nd hist:, dm; the dinner hour. She was in depressed spirits in %Peso quence of her mother speaking about Brooke. "I,7ita had remonstrated with her about her acquaintance no the youth,but he gave up doing so about seven Wee ago. He said to her then that she was too In„glijrare think about" sweethearting," and ought to OM' .41. of her parents and the younger members of the fact, The last time be saw Brooke was a svere„o fore last Monday, on returning to Battey tad cricket match at Bradford. His daughter wag v;lti, old lad, and he said to them, "You are on with ycnrA.... game again. They simply smiled, and did not sner Mrs. Brooke, widow, mother of the youth, nat,ga husband, Josiah Brooke, committed suicide faniera years ago. He drowned himself near the placer/pd. the bodies of her son and Florence Kilburn were le ffis, She had told her son he was too young to court tin&gt; and that he would have witness to keep when abe, sed not work ; also that she had had trouble enougur tO did not want any more making,nor did she waet, „11„11130111 trouble anyone else. She saw him with the gin evening of the 2nd inst., near the Clothiers' Arms, nip% ford-road.—Police-sergeant Musgrave deposed to finudir the body of Brooke. Upon searching the clothesb!ter covered a piece of paper, upon which was vi1;;.00 "Good-bye, mother. God bless you and the baby: to baby referred to was one belonging to the girl's sior,44 which he was very much attached.—Ellen day der44 that she was employed at lugs Mill with the den7os girl, and on the 2nd inst., she told witneis that strng had some words with her mother about Brooke, au alt that if she could not live with him she would die:ita him. Witness told her not to be silly, and parting .0, her saw Brooke join her.—A boat haul4,named Mar,rl r proved finding the body of the girl, and Detective tilico shaw said that in ber pocket was found a piece of Pio° bearing the words, "Good-bye mother • God bless 9,4 —The Coroner then summed up, and, 0011 the girl's remark to her companion in the go. said it aypeared to suggest that there had bee...n conversation between the unfortunate lovers beflxv pot on the subject of suicide. Brocke was the son 0f!.60 who had put an end to his own existence, but ic,nntie heredity affected him could not be sai I. As woos/ parents, they had only exercised their rightful antkl., al and neither of the young people had been ill-treateuo any way. Probably the young man had influenetal girl, and was not responsible at the tine, f•-r his —A verdidt of suicide whilst temporari:y insane 974' .turned in each case.</p>
